var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Main-functions-and-types","page":"Main functions and types","text":"Most uses of the package need only what is on this page.","title":"Main functions and types"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Setting-up-a-problem","page":"Main functions and types","text":"","title":"Setting up a problem"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Estimating","page":"Main functions and types","text":"","title":"Estimating"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#The-result-type","page":"Main functions and types","text":"","title":"The result type"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Errors","page":"Main functions and types","text":"","title":"Errors"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ODEParameterEstimation","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ODEParameterEstimation\n\nEstimate the parameters and initial conditions of an ODE model from time-series data.\n\nBuild a ParameterEstimationProblem from a ModelingToolkit system and your data, call estimate, and read the ParameterEstimationResults it returns. The manual is at https://orebas.github.io/ODEParameterEstimation.jl/dev/.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ODEParameterEstimation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationProblem-Tuple{ModelingToolkitBase.AbstractSystem, AbstractVector{<:Symbolics.Equation}}","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ParameterEstimationProblem(system, measured_quantities; data, true_values, name, solver)\n\nSet up an estimation problem from a ModelingToolkit System and the quantities that were measured.\n\nmeasured_quantities is a vector of equations such as [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x1 + x2]. Each left-hand side names one measured series; each right-hand side says what it measures in terms of the model's states and parameters.\n\nKeywords\n\ndata: the measurements. A NamedTuple, a dictionary, or anything with named columns such as a DataFrame, with the time points under t and one vector per measured quantity under its name, for example (t = times, y1 = first_series, y2 = second_series). All series share the time points. For series measured at different times, pass an ObservationData. Leave it out to simulate data with sample_problem_data.\ntrue_values: the parameter values and initial conditions that generated the data, as pairs such as [a => 0.4, x1 => 1.0], when they are known. They are needed only to simulate data and to report errors against the truth. Numbers the system already carries, as in @parameters a = 0.4, are used for anything not listed.\nname: a label for the problem. Defaults to the system's name.\nsolver: the ODE solver used to simulate the model while estimating. Defaults to package_wide_default_ode_solver.\n\nExample\n\nusing ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named model = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x2, D(x2) ~ b * x1], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(model, [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x2];\n    data = (t = times, y1 = first_series, y2 = second_series))\nresults = estimate(problem)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationProblem","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ParameterEstimationProblem\n\nA model, the quantities measured on it, and the measurements.\n\nBuild one from a ModelingToolkit System with ParameterEstimationProblem(system, measured_quantities; data). The example models in ODEParameterEstimation.Examples, such as lotka_volterra(), return ready-made problems without data.\n\nFields\n\nname: a label for the problem.\nmodel: the system, as an OrderedODESystem.\nmeasured_quantities: equations such as y1 ~ x1, one per measured series.\ndata_sample: the measurements, or nothing before any are attached. Either a dictionary with the time points under \"t\" and each series under the right-hand side of its measured quantity, or an ObservationData.\nsolver: the ODE solver used to simulate the model while estimating.\np_true, ic: the true parameter values and initial conditions, NaN where they are not known.\nrecommended_time_interval, unident_count: notes carried by the built-in example models. Estimation does not read them.\n\nThe struct can also be built by passing all nine fields in the order name, model, measured_quantities, data_sample, recommended_time_interval, solver, p_true, ic, unident_count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationProblem"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.sample_problem_data","page":"Main functions and types","text":"sample_problem_data(problem; options...) -> ParameterEstimationProblem\nsample_problem_data(problem, options::EstimationOptions)\n\nSimulate measurements for problem from its true parameter values and initial conditions, and return a copy of the problem that carries them.\n\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 51, time_interval = [0.0, 10.0], noise_level = 0.01)\n\nThe options that matter here are:\n\ndatasize: the number of time points.\ntime_interval: [start, stop]; the points are evenly spaced over it. An example model from ODEParameterEstimation.Examples has an interval of its own, which the keyword form uses when you give none.\nuneven_sampling and uneven_sampling_times: use these time points instead.\nnoise_level and noise_model: add Gaussian noise. :additive uses a standard deviation of noise_level times the mean absolute value of each series; :relative scales each value by 1 + noise_level * randn().\node_solver: the ODE solver for the simulation.\nseed: with an integer, the noise is the same on every call and the caller's default RNG is left untouched. With nothing (the default), noise is drawn from the caller's default RNG, so Random.seed! controls it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.sample_problem_data"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ObservationData","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ObservationData(series; initial_time = 0.0)\n\nMeasurements in which each series has its own time points. Pass one as data to ParameterEstimationProblem in place of a table.\n\nArguments\n\nseries: a vector of ObservationSeries.\ninitial_time: the time at which initial conditions are reported. It cannot be later than the first measurement.\n\nThe series have to overlap in time, because the equations are solved inside the interval that all of them cover. Gaps are not filled in and repeated measurements are not averaged. Uncertainty (compute_uncertainty) is not available for data of this kind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ObservationData"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ObservationSeries","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ObservationSeries(name, experiment, quantity, times, values; noise_std = nothing)\n\nOne measured series with its own time points.\n\nArguments\n\nname: a label for the series.\nexperiment: a label for the experiment it belongs to.\nquantity: what was measured, as an expression in the model's states, such as x1 or x1 + x2.\ntimes, values: the measurements. They are copied and sorted by time. A time may appear more than once.\nnoise_std: the standard deviation of the noise in each value, if you know it. It is kept with the series. Estimation does not use it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ObservationSeries"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.observation_times","page":"Main functions and types","text":"observation_times(data, quantity)\n\nThe times at which quantity was measured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.observation_times"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.estimate","page":"Main functions and types","text":"estimate(problem; options...) -> Vector{ParameterEstimationResult}\nestimate(problem, options::EstimationOptions)\n\nEstimate the parameters and initial conditions of problem from its data.\n\nReturns the solutions found, best fit first. There is usually one. There are several when the data cannot tell them apart, and each then fits equally well. The vector is empty if no solution was found.\n\nKeyword arguments are the fields of EstimationOptions, for example estimate(problem; seed = 1, progress = true).\n\nThese are the returned_results of analyze_parameter_estimation_problem. Call that function instead when you also want the error statistics against known true values or the uncertainty report, which is experimental.\n\nExample\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\nbest = first(results)\nbest.parameters      # OrderedDict of parameter => value\nbest.states          # initial conditions\nbest[a]              # one value, by symbol or by name (best[:a])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.estimate"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.analyze_parameter_estimation_problem","page":"Main functions and types","text":"analyze_parameter_estimation_problem(problem, options = EstimationOptions())\n\nEstimate the parameters and initial conditions of problem and return everything the run produced, as (raw_results, analysis, uq).\n\nestimate is the short form: it returns analysis.returned_results.\n\nReturns\n\nraw_results: every candidate solution the solver found, before ranking, with the identifiability findings. Mostly useful for debugging.\nanalysis: a named tuple.\nreturned_results holds the solutions, best fit first, as ParameterEstimationResults.\nbest_approximation_error is the fit error of the best one.\nbest_max_error, best_mean_error, best_median_error, best_min_error and best_rms_error are relative errors against the problem's true values, each taken as the best over all candidates. They are Inf when the true values are not known.\nalgebraic_multiplicity is the number of solutions the equations admit, when it was determined.\nuq: the uncertainty report when compute_uncertainty = true, otherwise nothing. Uncertainty estimation is experimental.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.analyze_parameter_estimation_problem"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.EstimationOptions","page":"Main functions and types","text":"EstimationOptions(; options...)\n\nEverything that can be adjusted about an estimation. Every option has a default, and the defaults are meant to be used: estimate(problem) is the normal call.\n\nOptions are given by keyword, either directly or through this struct:\n\nestimate(problem; seed = 1, progress = true)\n\noptions = EstimationOptions(seed = 1, progress = true)\nestimate(problem, options)\n\nThe options most people reach for come first. The rest adjust one stage of the method each, and are grouped by stage.\n\nEveryday options\n\ninterpolators: the curve fits used to estimate derivatives of the data, as a vector such as [InterpolatorAAAD]. Each is run and their candidate solutions are pooled. The default is nine methods: Gaussian processes, rational approximations and Chebyshev series. A shorter list is faster.\npolish_solutions (default true): refine each solution by least squares against the data. false returns the algebraic estimates as they are.\nopt_lb, opt_ub (default nothing): lower and upper bounds, as vectors with the states first and then the parameters. Refinement stays inside them.\nseed (default nothing): with an integer, repeated runs give identical results and Julia's default random number generator is left as it was. With nothing, random draws come from the default generator, so results vary a little from run to run unless you call Random.seed! first.\nprogress (default false): print one timestamped line as each stage starts and finishes.\nshooting_points (default 12): how many time points the equations are solved at. Fewer is faster.\nabstol, reltol (default 1e-14): tolerances for simulating the model.\n\nSimulated data\n\nThese are read only by sample_problem_data.\n\ndatasize (default 21): the number of time points.\ntime_interval (default [-0.5, 0.5]): the first and last time.\nnoise_level (default 0.0): the size of the Gaussian noise added.\nnoise_model (default :additive): with :additive, the noise has standard deviation noise_level times the mean absolute value of the series. With :relative, each value is multiplied by 1 + noise_level * randn().\nuneven_sampling (default false), uneven_sampling_times: sample at these times, of which there must be datasize, instead of on an even grid.\node_solver (default AutoVern9(Rodas5P())): the ODE solver for the simulation.\n\nOutput\n\nnooutput (default true): print nothing and pass only errors to the logger. false prints the stages and a summary of the results.\ndiagnostics (default false): print detailed diagnostics and write the candidate-synthesis log under artifacts/diagnostics/.\nsave_system (default false), save_filepath (default \"\"): save the polynomial systems to files, under save_filepath or else saved_systems/.\nheartbeat (default true): with nooutput = false, include the stage lines that progress prints.\nprofile_phases (default false): print the time and memory each stage took.\ndebug_solver, debug_cas_diagnostics, debug_dimensional_analysis (default false): extra output from the solver and the equation builder.\nhc_show_progress (default false): show HomotopyContinuation's progress bar.\ndump_raw_candidates_path, dump_polished_path (default nothing): write the candidate solutions to these CSV files, before and after refinement.\n\nEstimating derivatives\n\ncustom_interpolators (default Function[]): your own functions (times, values) -> curve, one for each InterpolatorCustom entry in interpolators, in order.\nauto_filter_interpolators (default true): leave out the rational interpolators when the data look noisy, because they amplify noise.\ns3_adapt_k (default 10.0): noise multiplier for the tolerance of the InterpolatorS3Adapt methods. Higher keeps fewer support points.\ngp_derivative_lengthscale_factor (default 1.0): multiplies the fitted length scale of InterpolatorAGPUQ. Values below one smooth less.\ngp_s3_refinement (default false): deprecated. List the InterpolatorS3Adapt methods in interpolators instead.\n\nBuilding and solving the equations\n\nflow (default FlowStandard): FlowStandard is the algebraic method. FlowDirectOpt fits by local optimization alone.\nsystem_solver (default SolverHC): how the polynomial systems are solved. SolverHC is homotopy continuation, which finds every solution. The others are local solvers and can miss solutions: SolverRobust is a trust-region method started from several points, and SolverFastNLOpt is Levenberg–Marquardt from one point.\nuse_si_template (default true): build the equations with StructuralIdentifiability.jl.\nsi_probability (default 0.99): the probability with which the identifiability analysis is correct.\nsi_fix_strategy (default :local_basis): how values are chosen for quantities that cannot be determined: :local_basis or :identifiable_functions.\nsi_placeholder_fail_categories (default Symbol[]): for development. Kinds of unresolved variable in the equations that should raise an error.\nsystem_construction_policy (default :noise_frontier): which equations are used. :noise_frontier prefers systems that need lower derivatives of the data and have fewer solutions. :legacy is the earlier choice.\nconstruction_candidate_limit (default 64), construction_beam_width (default 16), construction_compute_mixed_volume (default true): limits on the search that :noise_frontier does.\nshooting_warp (default true), shooting_warp_beta (default 3.0): place more of the shooting_points near the start of the data, and how strongly.\nuse_multipoint (default true), multipoint_n_points (default 2), multipoint_max_pairs (default 20), multipoint_pair_strategy (default :spread): also solve systems that combine equations from several time points. The strategy is :spread or :boundary_order.\nauto_handle_transcendentals (default true): rewrite sin(c*t), cos(c*t) and exp(c*t) in polynomial form.\nauto_rescale (default true): rescale states, parameters and data by powers of two to bring them near one, and convert the estimates back.\nuse_parameter_homotopy (default true): solve at one time point and track the solutions to the others, which is faster than solving each afresh.\nhomotopy_tracking_mode (default :generic_start): how that tracking is done: :generic_start, :gamma_straight, :gamma_straight_fallback or :parameter.\ngamma_max_seeds (default 5), gamma_seed (default 0): the number of random restarts for the tracking, and their seed. 0 uses a fixed seed for each problem, a positive value is used as given, and a negative value draws a new one every run, which seed cannot make repeatable.\nuse_column_scaling (default true): scale the unknowns of each polynomial system by the size of the data derivatives.\nhc_real_tol (default 1e-9): how small the imaginary part of a solution must be for it to count as real.\nhc_threading (default true), hc_compile_mode (default :all): passed to HomotopyContinuation: whether to use threads, and whether to compile the systems (:all, :none or :mixed).\n\nChoosing the solutions to return\n\nalgebraic_multiplicity (default nothing), compute_algebraic_multiplicity (default true): the number of solutions the equations have. It is worked out automatically unless you set it. At most this many results are returned.\nbranch_top_k (default 20): the most results returned when that number is not known. 0 returns all of them.\nrank_strategy (default :err_only): the order of the results. :err_only is by fit error. :sat_neg1_err, :sat_err, :lognorm_err and :lognorm_neg1_err are experimental alternatives.\nbranch_detection (default true): rank without using the true values. false ranks by distance to them, which is only meaningful in a benchmark.\nclustering_threshold (default 1e-5): the relative distance below which two candidates are the same solution.\ncluster_method (default :identifiable_subspace), rough_cluster_eps (default 1.0), subspace_cluster_eps (default 0.05): how near-duplicates are merged. :identifiable_subspace first separates candidates that are far apart, then merges close ones within each group, at these two distances. :bit_identical merges only at clustering_threshold.\nbranch_cluster_eps (default 0.001), branch_err_factor (default 100.0): before refinement, drop candidates whose fit error is more than branch_err_factor times the best, and merge the rest at this distance.\nbranch_diversity_selection (default true), branch_diversity_eps (default 0.01): when several results are returned, prefer ones that differ by at least this relative distance.\nbranch_completion (default true), branch_completion_max_anchors (default 1), branch_completion_residual_tol (default 1e-6): when the equations have several solutions, take the best one found and solve for the others from it.\nsynthesize_aggregate_candidates (default true): add candidates made by combining the others: the median, mean and trimmed mean of each value.\n\nRefining solutions\n\npolish_solver_solutions (default true): refine each solution of the polynomial equations against those equations.\npolish_solver_jacobian (default :forwarddiff), polish_solver_chunk_size (default 1): how the Jacobian for that is computed, :forwarddiff, :symbolic or :finitediff, and ForwardDiff's chunk size, where 0 chooses automatically.\npolish_method (default PolishLSOBoundedLog): the optimizer for polish_solutions. PolishLSOBoundedLog and PolishFastLMBoundedLog are Levenberg–Marquardt methods. PolishNewtonTrust, PolishBFGS and PolishLBFGS minimize the sum of squares directly.\npolish_maxiters (default 100), polish_maxtime (default 3600.0 seconds), polish_max_concurrency (default Threads.nthreads()): limits on the iterations and time spent on each solution, and on how many are refined at once.\npolish_divergence_factor (default 10.0), polish_stagnation_window (default 50): stop when the loss has grown by this factor, or has not improved for this many iterations.\npolish_ode_maxiters (default 5000): the step limit for the ODE solver during refinement.\npolish_coordinate_policy (default :auto): the coordinates refinement works in. :auto takes logarithms of quantities whose bounds are positive. The others are :linear, :log_only and :shifted_log_only.\npolish_regularization_lambda (default 0.0): the strength of an L2 penalty in those coordinates.\npolish_softwall_lambda (default 1e-2), polish_softwall_epsilon (default 0.10): a penalty that keeps values off their bounds: its strength, and the width of the band beside each bound where it acts, as a fraction of the range.\npolish_lso_delta (default 10.0), polish_lso_x_tol, polish_lso_f_tol, polish_lso_g_tol (default -1.0): the trust-region radius and tolerances of the Levenberg–Marquardt methods. A negative tolerance means abstol or reltol.\nopt_maxiters (default 10000): the iteration limit for the local optimization of FlowDirectOpt.\nopt_ad_backend (default :forward): how the optimizers get derivatives: :forward for ForwardDiff or :finite for finite differences.\nterminal_fallback (default :direct_opt): what to do when the algebraic method finds nothing. :direct_opt fits by local optimization, within polish_maxiters and polish_maxtime. :none returns no results.\nbacksolve_recovery (default :algebraic_resolve): what to do when a solution blows up as it is integrated back to the first time point. :algebraic_resolve keeps its parameters and solves for the states at that time again. :none does nothing.\nt0_state_completion (default :strict): with :seed_for_polish, when that recovery finds no states it supplies starting values for polish_solutions instead. With :strict it does not.\nuse_sensitivity_seeds (default false), sensitivity_seed_probe_scale (default 1.0), sensitivity_seed_eigenvalue_threshold (default 0.01), sensitivity_seed_mahalanobis_threshold (default -1.0), sensitivity_sigma_d_kappa (default 2.0): experimental. Add starting points for refinement along the directions in which the data constrain a solution least.\n\nUncertainty\n\nUncertainty estimation is experimental.\n\ncompute_uncertainty (default false): also estimate standard errors for the best solution. Needs InterpolatorAGPUQ in interpolators.\nuq_failure_policy (default :return_failed): when standard errors cannot be computed, :return_failed returns a UQUnavailable that says why, and :throw raises an error.\nuq_noise_source (default :learned_gp_homoscedastic): where the noise level comes from: the Gaussian process fit, or with :smoother_residual_edf the residuals of that fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.EstimationOptions"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationResult","page":"Main functions and types","text":"ParameterEstimationResult\n\nOne solution: parameter values and initial conditions with which the model reproduces the data.\n\nPrinting a result shows its values. result[a], result[:a] and result[\"a\"] look one up.\n\nFields\n\nparameters: the estimated parameter values, keyed by symbol.\nstates: the estimated states at report_time, keyed by symbol.\nreport_time: the time those states belong to: the first time point of the data, or the initial_time of an ObservationData.\nerr: the fit error, the sum of squared differences between the data and the simulated model over all series and time points. Results are ranked by it.\nall_unidentifiable: the parameters and initial conditions that the measured quantities cannot determine. Their reported values are arbitrary.\nunident_dict: the values those were fixed at while solving.\nsolution: the simulated trajectory.\ndatasize: the number of time points.\nat_time: the time point at which the equations were solved.\ninterpolator_source: the interpolator whose derivative estimates led here.\nprovenance: a ResultProvenance record of how the solution was produced.\nreturn_code: an older summary of the same: :algebraic, :direct_opt, or the name of a recovery path.\nbranch_size: how many candidate solutions were merged into this one.\n\nWhen the package rescales a badly scaled problem internally (auto_rescale), err, solution and unident_dict stay in the rescaled units. parameters and states are always in yours.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ParameterEstimationResult"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/api/#Base.getindex-Tuple{ParameterEstimationResult, Any}","page":"Main functions and types","text":"result[variable]\n\nThe estimated value of a parameter, or of a state's initial condition. variable is the symbol, or its name: result[a], result[:a] and result[\"a\"] are the same.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.UnsupportedModelClassError","page":"Main functions and types","text":"UnsupportedModelClassError\n\nThrown when a model is not rational: it applies a function such as sin, exp or sqrt to a state or to an unknown parameter. expressions lists the terms found, and category says which kind they are.\n\nsin(c * t), cos(c * t) and exp(c * t) with a number c depend on time alone and are accepted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UnsupportedModelClassError"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.UnsupportedDerivativeOrderError","page":"Main functions and types","text":"UnsupportedDerivativeOrderError\n\nThrown when a model needs a higher derivative of the measured data than the package computes (order 20). This happens when a few measured quantities have to account for many states and parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UnsupportedDerivativeOrderError"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#ODEParameterEstimation.SamplingFailureError","page":"Main functions and types","text":"SamplingFailureError\n\nThrown by sample_problem_data when the model cannot be simulated over the whole time interval, for example because the solution blows up.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.SamplingFailureError"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/how_it_works/#How-it-works","page":"How it works","text":"Most parameter-estimation tools search. They start from a guess, simulate the model, and adjust the guess to shrink the mismatch with the data. That works when the guess is good. When it is not, the search can settle on a wrong answer, and nothing tells you whether a different answer would fit just as well.\n\nThis package solves equations instead.","title":"How it works"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/how_it_works/#The-idea","page":"How it works","text":"Take a model with one parameter and one measured quantity:\n\nx(t) = -ax(t) qquad y(t) = x(t)\n\nDifferentiating the measurement gives y = x = -ay. So if the data tell us y and y at some time, then a = -yy. That is an equation for a, and solving it takes no search.\n\nThe same thing works in general. Differentiate the measured quantities a few times, use the model to rewrite each derivative of a state, and you get polynomial equations that link the unknown parameters and states to the values of y y y dots at a chosen time. Take enough derivatives and there are as many equations as unknowns.","title":"The idea"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/how_it_works/#The-steps","page":"How it works","text":"Find out what can be determined. StructuralIdentifiability.jl works out, from the model alone, which parameters and initial conditions the measured quantities determine. Anything they do not determine is given a fixed value and flagged in the result.\nEstimate derivatives from the data. Each measured series is fitted with a smooth curve, and the curve is differentiated. Several kinds of curve are used: Gaussian processes, which cope with noise, and rational and Chebyshev approximations, which are very accurate on clean data.\nBuild the polynomial equations and solve them. The derivative values go into the equations, at several time points and for each fitted curve. HomotopyContinuation.jl finds all the solutions of each system.\nTest every candidate against the data. Each real solution gives parameter values and initial conditions. The model is simulated with them and compared with the measurements.\nRefine. The candidates that come close are then fitted to the data by least squares, each starting from its own values. On exact data this changes nothing. On noisy data it is what makes the answer accurate.\nReport the distinct answers. Candidates that agree are merged, and the rest are ranked by how well they fit. The package works out how many solutions the equations have and returns at most that many.","title":"The steps"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/how_it_works/#What-follows-from-this","page":"How it works","text":"Every answer, not one. If two parameter sets explain the data equally well, you get both. See More than one answer.\nRational models only. The equations must be polynomial, so the model has to be built from polynomials and their ratios. See Which models work.\nDerivatives are the hard part. Noise grows with every derivative taken, so on noisy data the solutions of the equations are rough, and the refinement step does the rest. See Noisy data.","title":"What follows from this"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/how_it_works/#Papers","page":"How it works","text":"The method is described in\n\nO. Bassik, Y. Berman, S. Go, H. Hong, I. Ilmer, A. Ovchinnikov, C. Rackauckas, P. Soto, C. Yap. Robust parameter estimation for rational ordinary differential equations. Applied Mathematics and Computation 509 (2026). doi:10.1016/j.amc.2025.129638, arXiv:2303.02159.\n\nIts extension to noisy data, which estimates the derivatives with Gaussian processes, is described in\n\nO. Bassik, A. Demin, A. Ovchinnikov. Practical algebraic parameter estimation for noisy data via Gaussian process regression. arXiv:2609.30451 (2026).","title":"Papers"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"This page fits a model to data from start to finish. Most of the time it takes is Julia installing and compiling packages.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Installing","page":"Getting started","text":"You need Julia 1.12 or later. The package is not in the General registry yet, so add it from GitHub, along with ModelingToolkit, which models are written in:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/orebas/ODEParameterEstimation.jl\")\nPkg.add(\"ModelingToolkit\")","title":"Installing"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#The-whole-thing","page":"Getting started","text":"using ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n# A predator-prey model with four unknown rates.\n@parameters α β γ δ\n@variables prey(t) predators(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named lotka_volterra = System([\n    D(prey) ~ α * prey - β * prey * predators,\n    D(predators) ~ δ * prey * predators - γ * predators,\n], t)\n\n# Both populations were counted. Here the counts are simulated from known values.\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(lotka_volterra, [y1 ~ prey, y2 ~ predators];\n    true_values = [α => 1.1, β => 0.4, γ => 0.4, δ => 0.1, prey => 1.0, predators => 0.5])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 101, time_interval = [0.0, 10.0])\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\nresults[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    α = 1.1\n    β = 0.4\n    γ = 0.4\n    δ = 0.1\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    prey(t) = 1\n    predators(t) = 0.5\n  Fit error: 6.94e-26\n\nThe estimates are the values the data were simulated from.","title":"The whole thing"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#What-each-part-does","page":"Getting started","text":"The model is an ordinary ModelingToolkit System. t is time and D is the derivative with respect to it. If you have a model written for simulation with ModelingToolkit, you can pass that same system.\n\nThe measured quantities, [y1 ~ prey, y2 ~ predators], say what was observed. Each left-hand side is a name you choose for one series of measurements. Each right-hand side says what that series measures. It does not have to be a single state: if only the total number of animals had been counted, you would write [y ~ prey + predators].\n\nThe data here come from sample_problem_data, which simulates the model with the true_values and records the measured quantities at 101 times. With real measurements you skip both and pass the numbers themselves:\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(lotka_volterra, [y1 ~ prey, y2 ~ predators];\n    data = (t = times, y1 = prey_counts, y2 = predator_counts))\n\nestimate returns a vector of results, best fit first. There is usually one. More than one answer explains when there are several.\n\nThe first time a model is estimated in a Julia session, Julia compiles code for that model. For this one that takes under a minute. Calling it again takes seconds, with the same data or with new data.","title":"What each part does"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Reading-the-result","page":"Getting started","text":"A result prints its values, and you can take them out one at a time or all together:\n\nbest = results[1]\nbest[α]\n1.0999999999999623\n\nbest.parameters\nOrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbolics.Num, Float64} with 4 entries:\n  α => 1.1\n  β => 0.4\n  γ => 0.4\n  δ => 0.1\n\nbest.states holds the initial conditions in the same form. Both are dictionaries keyed by the symbols of your model.","title":"Reading the result"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Seeing-the-fit","page":"Getting started","text":"To see how well an estimate explains the data, simulate the model with it and draw both. This is plain ModelingToolkit, OrdinaryDiffEq and Plots, which you may need to add first:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, Plots\n\nfitted = solve(ODEProblem(mtkcompile(lotka_volterra), merge(best.states, best.parameters), (0.0, 10.0)), Tsit5())\n\ndata = problem.data_sample   # the simulated measurements\nscatter(data[\"t\"], [data[prey] data[predators]]; label = [\"prey, measured\" \"predators, measured\"], markersize = 2)\nplot!(fitted; idxs = [prey, predators], label = [\"prey, fitted\" \"predators, fitted\"], xlabel = \"time\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=4}","title":"Seeing the fit"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Where-next","page":"Getting started","text":"Your own data: vectors, tables, and series measured at different times.\nNoisy data: what to expect when the measurements are not exact, and what helps.\nMore than one answer: models where the data allow several parameter sets, or leave some parameters open.\nWhich models work: the kinds of equations the method can handle.","title":"Where next"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#Results","page":"Results","text":"estimate returns a vector of ParameterEstimationResults, best fit first.\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named oscillator = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x2, D(x2) ~ b * x1], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(oscillator, [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x2];\n    true_values = [a => 0.4, b => 0.9, x1 => 1.0, x2 => 0.5])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 41, time_interval = [0.0, 5.0])\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\n1-element Vector{ParameterEstimationResult}:\n ParameterEstimationResult(a = 0.4, b = 0.9; x1(t) = 1, x2(t) = 0.5)","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#One-result","page":"Results","text":"best = results[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.4\n    b = 0.9\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 1\n    x2(t) = 0.5\n  Fit error: 3.45e-26\n\nThe printout has the parameters, the initial conditions with the time they belong to, and the fit error. The values can be taken out by symbol or by name:\n\nbest[a], best[:b], best[\"x1\"]\n(0.4000000000000009, 0.8999999999999815, 1.0000000000000009)\n\nor all at once, as dictionaries keyed by symbol:\n\nbest.parameters\nOrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbolics.Num, Float64} with 2 entries:\n  a => 0.4\n  b => 0.9\n\nbest.states\nOrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbolics.Num, Float64} with 2 entries:\n  x1(t) => 1.0\n  x2(t) => 0.5\n\nbest.err is the fit error: the sum of squared differences between the data and the model simulated with these values. It is what results are ranked by. Use it to compare the results of one run with each other.","title":"One result"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#Using-the-estimates","page":"Results","text":"best.states and best.parameters are in the form ModelingToolkit takes, so simulating the fitted model is one line:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\n\nfitted = solve(ODEProblem(mtkcompile(oscillator), merge(best.states, best.parameters), (0.0, 5.0)), Tsit5())\n\nSeeing the fit plots such a simulation against the data.","title":"Using the estimates"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#Several-results","page":"Results","text":"When the vector holds more than one result, each is a different set of values that fits the data. When a result marks some values not identifiable, the data do not determine them. More than one answer covers both.\n\nAn empty vector means no solution was found. See Troubleshooting.","title":"Several results"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#When-the-true-values-are-known","page":"Results","text":"For testing a model or the method, analyze_parameter_estimation_problem returns the results together with a comparison against the problem's true values:\n\nraw, analysis, uq = analyze_parameter_estimation_problem(problem, EstimationOptions())\nanalysis.best_max_error\n3.7969627442180354e-14\n\nanalysis.returned_results is what estimate returns. best_max_error is the largest relative error over the parameters and initial conditions, for the candidate solution that came closest to the truth. That candidate is usually the first result, but it does not have to be, so to score the first result itself, compare its values with the truth directly.","title":"When the true values are known"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/results/#Where-a-result-came-from","page":"Results","text":"Each result records how it was produced. best.interpolator_source names the curve fit whose derivative estimates led to it, and best.provenance holds the rest: which time point it was solved at and whether it was refined afterwards.","title":"Where a result came from"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Example-models","page":"Example models","text":"The package comes with ready-made problems to try things on. They live in a submodule, ODEParameterEstimation.Examples. Each is a function that returns a ParameterEstimationProblem with true values and no data, so the usual pattern is:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation\nusing ODEParameterEstimation.Examples\n\nproblem = sample_problem_data(lotka_volterra(); datasize = 101)\nresults = estimate(problem)\n\nEvery model carries a time interval that suits it, and sample_problem_data uses it unless you pass time_interval yourself.\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation\nusing ODEParameterEstimation.Examples\n\nfunction describe(group)\n    println(rpad(\"model\", 32), \"states  parameters  measured  interval\")\n    for name in example_models(group)\n        problem = example_problem(name)\n        println(rpad(name, 32), rpad(length(problem.ic), 8), rpad(length(problem.p_true), 12),\n            rpad(length(problem.measured_quantities), 10), problem.recommended_time_interval)\n    end\nend","title":"Example models"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Looking-inside-one","page":"Example models","text":"A problem carries its ModelingToolkit system, its measured quantities and its true values:\n\nusing ModelingToolkit\n\nproblem = lotka_volterra()\nequations(problem.model.system)\n2-element Vector{Symbolics.Equation}:\n Differential(t, 1)(r(t)) ~ k1*r(t) - k2*r(t)*w(t)\n Differential(t, 1)(w(t)) ~ -k3*w(t) + k2*r(t)*w(t)\n\nproblem.measured_quantities\n1-element Vector{Symbolics.Equation}:\n y1(t) ~ r(t)\n\nproblem.p_true\nOrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbolics.Num, Float64} with 3 entries:\n  k1 => 1.0\n  k2 => 0.5\n  k3 => 0.3","title":"Looking inside one"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#First-steps","page":"Example models","text":"Small models that are solved quickly and accurately.\n\ndescribe(:first_steps)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\nharmonic_oscillator             2       2           2         [0.0, 10.0]\noscillator_mixed_outputs        2       2           2         [0.0, 10.0]\nexponential_decay               1       1           1         [0.0, 10.0]\ndecay_cubed_output              1       1           1         [0.0, 10.0]\nforced_decay                    1       2           1         [0.0, 10.0]\nlotka_volterra                  2       3           1         [0.0, 20.0]\nvanderpol                       2       2           2         [0.0, 10.0]\nbrusselator                     2       2           2         [0.0, 20.0]\ndaisy_mamil3                    3       5           2         [0.0, 10.0]\nrepressilator                   6       3           3         [0.0, 24.0]","title":"First steps"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Something-cannot-be-determined","page":"Example models","text":"In each of these the measured quantities leave at least one parameter or initial condition open. See More than one answer.\n\ndescribe(:cannot_be_determined)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\nsum_of_rates                    1       2           1         [0.0, 10.0]\npartly_observed_chain           3       4           2         [0.0, 10.0]\nhidden_scale                    3       3           1         [0.0, 10.0]\ntreatment                       4       5           2         [0.0, 40.0]\ntwo_compartment_pk              2       5           1         [0.0, 48.0]\nmass_spring_damper              2       4           1         [0.0, 10.0]","title":"Something cannot be determined"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Several-answers","page":"Example models","text":"Two models in which several sets of values fit the data equally well, each beside a version that is measured fully enough to have one answer.\n\ndescribe(:several_answers)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\nhidden_subpopulations           5       6           3         [0.0, 12.0]\nhidden_subpopulations_measured  5       6           5         [0.0, 12.0]\nreceptor_subtypes               3       6           2         [0.0, 8.0]\nreceptor_subtypes_measured      3       6           3         [0.0, 8.0]","title":"Several answers"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Larger-models","page":"Example models","text":"Most of these are benchmarks from the identifiability literature. They take longer, some of them minutes, and two are beyond what the package solves in reasonable time at present. Their descriptions say which.\n\ndescribe(:larger_models)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\ncrauste                         5       13          4         [0.0, 25.0]\ndaisy_ex3                       4       5           2         [0.0, 10.0]\ndaisy_mamil4                    4       7           3         [0.0, 10.0]\nfitzhugh_nagumo                 2       3           1         [0.0, 10.0]\nhiv                             5       10          4         [0.0, 25.0]\nbiohydrogenation                4       6           2         [0.0, 36.0]\nseir                            4       3           2         [0.0, 60.0]\nsirsforced                      5       6           2         [0.0, 30.0]\nslowfast                        6       2           4         [0.0, 10.0]\nallee_competition               2       8           2         [0.0, 10.0]","title":"Larger models"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Control-systems","page":"Example models","text":"Systems driven by a known sinusoidal input. See Inputs that depend on time.\n\ndescribe(:control_systems)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\ndc_motor                        2       2           1         [0.0, 5.0]\nquadrotor_altitude              2       2           1         [0.0, 10.0]\nforced_lotka_volterra           2       4           2         [0.0, 5.0]\nmagnetic_levitation             3       3           1         [0.0, 5.0]\naircraft_pitch                  3       4           1         [0.0, 10.0]\nbicycle_model                   2       4           2         [0.0, 20.0]\nboost_converter                 2       3           2         [0.0, 0.5]\nbilinear_system                 2       8           2         [0.0, 15.0]","title":"Control systems"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#A-model-that-has-to-be-rewritten","page":"Example models","text":"One tank, written as it comes and written so that the package accepts it. See Rewriting a model so that it is accepted.\n\ndescribe(:rewriting)\nmodel                           states  parameters  measured  interval\ntank_level                      1       2           1         [0.0, 30.0]\ntank_level_rewritten            1       2           1         [0.0, 30.0]","title":"A model that has to be rewritten"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/example_models/#Listing-them","page":"Example models","text":"","title":"Listing them"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.brusselator-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"brusselator()\n\nThe Brusselator, a two-species chemical oscillator with a cubic term, with both concentrations measured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.brusselator"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_mamil3-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"daisy_mamil3()\n\nA linear three-compartment model with five exchange rates, a standard identifiability benchmark. Two of the three compartments are measured.\n\nAll five rates and the unmeasured initial amount are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_mamil3"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.decay_cubed_output-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"decay_cubed_output()\n\nThe decay of exponential_decay, measured through its cube: the measured quantity is x1^3. A measured quantity may be any rational function of the states and parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.decay_cubed_output"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.exponential_decay-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"exponential_decay()\n\nOne state that decays, x1' = -a x1, measured directly. The simplest model there is: one parameter and one initial condition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.exponential_decay"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.forced_decay-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"forced_decay()\n\nA decaying state pushed by a known sinusoid, x' = -a x + b sin(0.5 t), measured directly. The smallest model with an input that depends on time. See Inputs that depend on time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.forced_decay"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.harmonic_oscillator-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"harmonic_oscillator()\n\nTwo states that drive each other, x1' = -a x2 and x2' = b x1, both measured. The smallest model with two parameters.\n\nBoth parameters and both initial conditions are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.harmonic_oscillator"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.lotka_volterra-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"lotka_volterra()\n\nThe Lotka–Volterra predator-prey model with three rates. Only the prey r is measured.\n\nThe three rates and the unmeasured initial number of predators are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.lotka_volterra"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.oscillator_mixed_outputs-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"oscillator_mixed_outputs()\n\nThe equations of harmonic_oscillator, with neither state measured on its own: the two measured quantities are 2 x1 + 0.5 x2 and 3 x1 - 0.25 x2.\n\nEverything is still recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.oscillator_mixed_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.repressilator-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"repressilator()\n\nA ring of three genes, each repressing the next, with saturating repression terms of the form beta / (1 + n p). The three proteins are measured and the three mRNA levels are not.\n\nA model with parameters in denominators. It takes several minutes with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.repressilator"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.vanderpol-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"vanderpol()\n\nThe van der Pol oscillator, x1' = a x2 and x2' = -x1 - b (x1^2 - 1) x2, with both states measured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.vanderpol"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_scale-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"hidden_scale()\n\nTwo unmeasured states feed a third, x3' = c (x1 + x2), and only x3 is measured. Scaling x1 and x2 up and c down by the same factor changes nothing that is measured.\n\nThe rates a and b and x3(0) are determined. c, x1(0) and x2(0) are reported as undetermined. Two results are returned, one for each way of assigning the two rates to the two hidden states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.mass_spring_damper-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"mass_spring_damper()\n\nA mass on a spring with a damper, pushed by a constant unknown force: m x'' + c x' + k x = F. The position is measured.\n\nDividing the equation by m shows that only the ratios c/m, k/m and F/m matter. All four parameters are reported as undetermined, and the initial position and velocity are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.mass_spring_damper"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.partly_observed_chain-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"partly_observed_chain()\n\nThree states, two of them measured. x2 grows at the rate b + c, so only that sum is determined, and x3 is neither measured nor felt by anything that is, so d and its initial value are open.\n\na and the two measured initial conditions are recovered. b, c, d and x3(0) are reported as undetermined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.partly_observed_chain"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.sum_of_rates-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"sum_of_rates()\n\nGrowth at the rate a + b: x1' = (a + b) x1, with x1 measured. Only the sum can be determined, so a and b are both reported as undetermined. The smallest example of that.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.sum_of_rates"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.treatment-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"treatment()\n\nAn epidemic model with treatment: susceptible S, infected In, treated Tr and the constant population N. Only the treated and the population size are measured.\n\nMost of the model is left open by those two measurements: a, b, d, g, In(0) and S(0) are reported as undetermined, and nu is recovered. It takes a long time with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.treatment"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.two_compartment_pk-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"two_compartment_pk()\n\nA drug that moves between a central and a peripheral compartment and is eliminated from the central one. Only the central concentration is measured.\n\nOne concentration curve cannot separate the five parameters: all of them and the peripheral initial concentration are reported as undetermined. Only the central initial concentration is recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.two_compartment_pk"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_subpopulations-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"hidden_subpopulations()\n\nAn epidemic in which the infected fall into three groups, each with its own infection rate b and recovery rate a. The susceptible, the recovered and the total number infected are measured, so the three groups are never seen apart.\n\nNothing in the data says which group is which. There are six answers, one for each ordering of the three groups, and all six fit equally well. Compare hidden_subpopulations_measured. It takes a long time with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_subpopulations"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_subpopulations_measured-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"hidden_subpopulations_measured()\n\nThe model of hidden_subpopulations with each of the three infected groups measured on its own. There is one answer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hidden_subpopulations_measured"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.receptor_subtypes-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"receptor_subtypes()\n\nA ligand L that binds two receptor subtypes, each with its own amount and its own binding and release rates. The free ligand and the total amount bound are measured.\n\nThe two subtypes can be exchanged, so there are two answers. Compare receptor_subtypes_measured.\n\nwarning: Warning\nThe package does not solve this model in reasonable time at present. It is here as a model to work on, and no test estimates it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.receptor_subtypes"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.receptor_subtypes_measured-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"receptor_subtypes_measured()\n\nThe model of receptor_subtypes with the amount bound to each subtype measured on its own. There is one answer, and it is found quickly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.receptor_subtypes_measured"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.allee_competition-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"allee_competition()\n\nTwo competing species, each with logistic growth and an Allee threshold below which it declines. Both populations are measured. Eight parameters, with the carrying capacities in denominators.\n\nThere are four answers that fit the data equally well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.allee_competition"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.biohydrogenation-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"biohydrogenation()\n\nA chain of three reactions with saturating rates of the form k x / (K + x). The first two of the four concentrations are measured.\n\nThe final product x7 affects nothing that is measured, so x7(0) is reported as undetermined. There are two answers for the rest. It takes several minutes with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.biohydrogenation"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.crauste-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"crauste()\n\nThe Crauste model of the CD8 T-cell response to infection: naive cells N, early and late effector cells E and L, memory cells M and the pathogen P, with thirteen rates. N, E, P and the sum L + M are measured.\n\nwarning: Warning\nThe package does not solve this model in reasonable time at present. It is here as a model to work on, and no test estimates it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.crauste"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_ex3-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"daisy_ex3()\n\nA linear three-state model driven by a ramp input u0, from the examples of the DAISY identifiability software. x1 and the input are measured.\n\nThere are two answers. It takes several minutes with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_ex3"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_mamil4-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"daisy_mamil4()\n\nA linear four-compartment model with seven exchange rates. x1, x2 and the sum x3 + x4 are measured.\n\nCompartments 3 and 4 are seen only together and can be exchanged, so there are two answers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.daisy_mamil4"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.fitzhugh_nagumo-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"fitzhugh_nagumo()\n\nThe FitzHugh–Nagumo model of a nerve cell: a fast voltage V with a cubic term and a slow recovery variable R. Only the voltage is measured.\n\nThe three parameters and the unmeasured R(0) are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.fitzhugh_nagumo"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hiv-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"hiv()\n\nA model of HIV infection and the immune response to it: uninfected cells x, infected cells y, virus v, and immune cells w and z, with ten rates. Four quantities are measured, one of them the sum y + v.\n\nThe values span eight orders of magnitude, from 2e-5 to 1000. The package rescales the problem internally (auto_rescale). It takes several minutes with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.hiv"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.seir-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"seir()\n\nThe SEIR epidemic model: susceptible S, exposed E, infected In and the constant population N. The infected and the population size are measured.\n\nThere are two answers: the data cannot tell the recovery rate a from the rate nu at which the exposed become infected. It takes several minutes with the default options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.seir"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.sirsforced-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"sirsforced()\n\nAn SIRS epidemic model whose infection rate varies with the seasons. The seasonal factor is produced by two extra states, x1 and x2, that form an oscillator of unknown frequency M. The infected and the recovered are measured.\n\nwarning: Warning\nThe package does not solve this model in reasonable time at present. It is here as a model to work on, and no test estimates it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.sirsforced"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.slowfast-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"slowfast()\n\nA chain of two reactions, A to B to C, watched through an absorbance signal eA xA + eB xB + eC xC. The concentration of C, the signal and two of the three coefficients are measured. The coefficients are constants, written as states that do not change.\n\nThere are two answers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.slowfast"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.aircraft_pitch-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"aircraft_pitch()\n\nThe short-period pitch dynamics of an aircraft: pitch angle theta, pitch rate q and angle of attack alpha. The elevator moves as 0.05 sin(2 t) and the pitch rate is measured.\n\nThe four aerodynamic coefficients are recovered. The pitch angle itself affects nothing that is measured, so theta(0) is reported as undetermined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.aircraft_pitch"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.bicycle_model-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"bicycle_model()\n\nThe lateral dynamics of a car as a single-track (\"bicycle\") model: lateral velocity vy and yaw rate r, with the steering angle 0.05 sin(0.5 t). Both states are measured. The geometry and the speed are taken as known.\n\nThe tyre stiffnesses Cf and Cr enter only divided by the mass or by the yaw inertia, so only those ratios are determined. All four parameters are reported as undetermined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.bicycle_model"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.bilinear_system-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"bilinear_system()\n\nA two-state system in which the input u = 1 + 0.5 sin(2 t) both adds to the states and multiplies them. Both states are measured.\n\nAll eight coefficients are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.bilinear_system"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.boost_converter-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"boost_converter()\n\nThe averaged model of a boost converter: inductor current iL and capacitor voltage vC, with the duty cycle varying as 0.5 + 0.1 sin(100 t). Both states are measured and the input voltage is taken as known.\n\nThe inductance, capacitance and load resistance are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.boost_converter"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.dc_motor-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"dc_motor()\n\nA DC motor: the armature current i produces a torque that turns the rotor at speed omega_m. The supply voltage is 12 + 2 sin(5 t) and the speed is measured. The electrical constants and the torque constant are taken as known.\n\nThe rotor inertia J and the friction coefficient b are recovered. With the torque constant unknown as well, only the ratios Kt/J and b/J could be determined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.dc_motor"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.forced_lotka_volterra-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"forced_lotka_volterra()\n\nA predator-prey model whose prey is harvested and restocked through the seasons, at the rate 0.3 sin(2 t). Both populations are measured.\n\nAll four rates are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.forced_lotka_volterra"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.magnetic_levitation-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"magnetic_levitation()\n\nA ball held by an electromagnet, linearised about its resting point: position x, velocity v and coil current i. The coil voltage is 5 + sin(5 t) and only the position is measured. The electrical constants are taken as known.\n\nThe effective mass, stiffness and damping are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.magnetic_levitation"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.quadrotor_altitude-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"quadrotor_altitude()\n\nThe altitude z of a quadrotor with linear drag, driven by a net vertical force 2 sin(t). The altitude is measured.\n\nThe mass m and the drag coefficient d are recovered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.quadrotor_altitude"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.tank_level-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"tank_level()\n\nA tank that drains through a hole in its floor. The level h falls at a rate proportional to sqrt(h), and the inflow 0.4 + 0.15 sin(0.5 t) is known. The level is measured.\n\nsqrt(h) is not a rational function of the state, so estimate refuses this model with an UnsupportedModelClassError. tank_level_rewritten is the same tank in a form that is accepted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.tank_level"},{"category":"method","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.tank_level_rewritten-Tuple{}","page":"Example models","text":"tank_level_rewritten()\n\nThe tank of tank_level with the state z = sqrt(h) in place of the level. Then z' = (Qin - k z) / (2 A z), which is rational, and the measured level is z^2. The data are the same.\n\nThe area A and the outflow coefficient k are recovered. A second result has z and k both negated: it gives the same level, and is the price of measuring z only through its square.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.tank_level_rewritten"},{"category":"module","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples","page":"Example models","text":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples\n\nReady-made problems to try the package on. Each model is a function that returns a ParameterEstimationProblem with true values and no data:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation\nusing ODEParameterEstimation.Examples\n\nproblem = sample_problem_data(lotka_volterra(); datasize = 101)\nresults = estimate(problem)\n\nexample_models lists them and example_groups lists the groups they are sorted into.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples"},{"category":"function","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_models","page":"Example models","text":"example_models() -> Vector{Symbol}\nexample_models(group::Symbol) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe names of the example models, all of them or those of one group.\n\nExample\n\nexample_models(:first_steps)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_models"},{"category":"function","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_groups","page":"Example models","text":"example_groups() -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe groups the example models are sorted into: :first_steps, :cannot_be_determined, :several_answers, :larger_models, :control_systems and :rewriting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_groups"},{"category":"function","location":"guides/example_models/#ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_problem","page":"Example models","text":"example_problem(name::Symbol) -> ParameterEstimationProblem\n\nThe example model called name: example_problem(:lotka_volterra) is lotka_volterra().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.Examples.example_problem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy_data/#Noisy-data","page":"Noisy data","text":"Measured data are noisy, and noise is hard on the equation-solving part of this method, because it works from derivatives of the data and differentiating amplifies noise. So estimate does not stop there. It takes each solution of the equations and fits it to the data by least squares. This page shows what each step contributes.","title":"Noisy data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy_data/#An-example","page":"Noisy data","text":"The predator-prey model again, with 101 measurements of each population. Noise has been added to them, with a standard deviation of 2% of each population's average size:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters α β γ δ\n@variables prey(t) predators(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named lotka_volterra = System([\n    D(prey) ~ α * prey - β * prey * predators,\n    D(predators) ~ δ * prey * predators - γ * predators,\n], t)\n\ntruth = [α => 1.1, β => 0.4, γ => 0.4, δ => 0.1, prey => 1.0, predators => 0.5]\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(lotka_volterra, [y1 ~ prey, y2 ~ predators]; true_values = truth)\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem;\n    datasize = 101, time_interval = [0.0, 10.0], noise_level = 0.02, seed = 1)\n\nHere is the estimate, and beside it what the equations gave before the fit, which polish_solutions = false returns:\n\nrefined = estimate(problem; seed = 1)[1]\nrough = estimate(problem; seed = 1, polish_solutions = false)[1]\n\nprintln(\"              true     rough   refined\")\nfor (quantity, value) in truth\n    println(rpad(quantity, 13), rpad(value, 9), rpad(round(rough[quantity]; sigdigits = 3), 8), round(refined[quantity]; sigdigits = 3))\nend\n              true     rough   refined\nα            1.1      0.983   1.1\nβ            0.4      0.388   0.401\nγ            0.4      0.471   0.401\nδ            0.1      0.121   0.101\nprey(t)      1.0      1.07    0.997\npredators(t) 0.5      0.351   0.491\n\nThe rough values come from derivatives of noisy data. They are in the right region, and some are well off. The refined ones started there and were then fitted to the data, and they are within a few percent of the truth.\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq, Plots\n\nfit = solve(ODEProblem(mtkcompile(lotka_volterra), merge(refined.states, refined.parameters), (0.0, 10.0)), Tsit5())\n\ndata = problem.data_sample\nscatter(data[\"t\"], [data[prey] data[predators]]; label = [\"prey, measured\" \"predators, measured\"], markersize = 2)\nplot!(fit; idxs = [prey, predators], label = [\"prey, fitted\" \"predators, fitted\"], xlabel = \"time\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=4}","title":"An example"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy_data/#Why-two-steps","page":"Noisy data","text":"Fitting by least squares is the standard way to estimate parameters, and its weakness is that it has to start near the answer. Solving the equations supplies that start, and because it finds every solution, the fit begins in the right place each time.\n\nOn exact data the solutions of the equations are already exact, and the fit leaves them as they are.","title":"Why two steps"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy_data/#What-else-helps","page":"Noisy data","text":"More points. The fit averages the noise over all of them, so more points mean a better answer.\nBounds. If you know the values are positive, or lie in some range, pass opt_lb and opt_ub. See Options.\nMeasuring more. A model whose unknowns all show up in the first or second derivative of the data gives a far better start than one that needs the fifth. Measuring one more state can make that difference.\n\nA run on noisy data can be made faster by using a single Gaussian-process interpolator, which here gives the same answer:\n\nestimate(problem; interpolators = [InterpolatorAGPRobust])","title":"What else helps"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/noisy_data/#How-good-is-the-answer","page":"Noisy data","text":"Plot it, as above. For a number, there is an experimental way to estimate a standard error for each parameter. See Uncertainty.","title":"How good is the answer"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Options","page":"Options","text":"estimate(problem) uses defaults that are meant to work as they are. This page covers the few options worth knowing about. EstimationOptions lists them all.\n\nOptions are keywords of estimate:\n\nresults = estimate(problem; seed = 1, progress = true)\n\nTo use the same options more than once, build them first:\n\noptions = EstimationOptions(seed = 1, progress = true)\nresults = estimate(problem, options)","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Watching-a-long-run","page":"Options","text":"A run prints nothing. With progress = true it prints a line as each stage starts and finishes:\n\n[14:02:11] ▶ Setup (identifiability)\n[14:02:11] ✓ Setup (identifiability) (0.5s)\n[14:02:11] ▶ SI Template (SIAN analysis)\n[14:02:17] ✓ SI Template (SIAN analysis) (5.9s)\n[14:02:17] ▶ Equation construction + Solving\n[14:02:17] ▶ interpolator 1/9 agp_robust","title":"Watching a long run"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Going-faster","page":"Options","text":"The first time a model is estimated in a Julia session, Julia compiles code for it. Nothing here changes that, and the second call shows the real speed.\n\nAfter that, most of the time goes into solving the polynomial equations once for every curve fit and every time point. Two options reduce the count.\n\ninterpolators chooses the curve fits. The default uses nine, and one is often enough:\n\nestimate(problem; interpolators = [InterpolatorAAAD])        # exact or nearly exact data\nestimate(problem; interpolators = [InterpolatorAGPRobust])   # noisy data\n\nshooting_points is the number of time points the equations are solved at. The default is 12.\n\nestimate(problem; shooting_points = 4)\n\nBoth trade some reliability for speed. The best answer is chosen from all the attempts, and with fewer attempts there is less to choose from.","title":"Going faster"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Refining-estimates-from-noisy-data","page":"Options","text":"Each answer is refined by least squares against the data, starting from the algebraic estimate. Noisy data shows what that buys. polish_solutions = false turns it off and returns the algebraic estimates as they are.\n\nIf you know the range the values must lie in, opt_lb and opt_ub keep the refinement inside it. Each is a vector with the states first and the parameters after them, in the order ModelingToolkit lists them: unknowns(problem.model.system), then parameters(problem.model.system).","title":"Refining estimates from noisy data"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Repeatable-results","page":"Options","text":"Several steps draw random numbers, so two runs on the same data can differ in the last digits, and now and then in which answer comes first. A seed makes runs repeat exactly:\n\nestimate(problem; seed = 1)\n\nsample_problem_data takes the same option, to fix the noise it adds. The seed does not touch Julia's default random number generator, so your own rand calls are unaffected.","title":"Repeatable results"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Standard-errors","page":"Options","text":"compute_uncertainty = true adds standard errors for the best answer. This part of the package is experimental. See Uncertainty.","title":"Standard errors"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#The-ODE-solver","page":"Options","text":"Candidate answers are tested by simulating the model. The solver for that belongs to the problem:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(model, measured; data, solver = Rodas5P())\n\nThe default, AutoVern9(Rodas5P()), switches to a stiff method when it needs to. abstol and reltol, both 1e-14 by default, are its tolerances.","title":"The ODE solver"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/options/#Seeing-what-the-package-is-doing","page":"Options","text":"nooutput = false prints the package's own account of a run: the stages, the candidates and a summary table. diagnostics = true adds much more and writes a log file. Both were built for developing the package, and neither is needed to use it.","title":"Seeing what the package is doing"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/functions/#Other-functions","page":"Other functions","text":"The remaining exported functions and constants, as they are documented in the source. The functions meant for everyday use are on Main functions and types.","title":"Other functions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/functions/#Constants","page":"Other functions","text":"","title":"Constants"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.aaad-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}, Bool}} where T","page":"Other functions","text":"aaad(xs::AbstractArray{T}, ys::AbstractArray{T}, force_gpr::Bool=false) -> AAADapprox\n\nStandard interpolation function using AAA algorithm. The force_gpr parameter is kept for backward compatibility but is not used.\n\nArguments\n\nxs::AbstractArray{T}: X coordinates\nys::AbstractArray{T}: Y coordinates (function values)\nforce_gpr::Bool: Unused parameter (for backward compatibility)\n\nReturns\n\nAAADapprox interpolator object that can be called as a function\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.aaad"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.aaad_gpr_pivot-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}}} where T","page":"Other functions","text":"aaad_gpr_pivot(xs::AbstractArray{T}, ys::AbstractArray{T}) -> GPRapprox\n\nCreates an interpolation function using Gaussian Process Regression. Normalizes input data, applies GPR with optimized hyperparameters, and returns a callable interpolator.\n\nArguments\n\nxs::AbstractArray{T}: X coordinates\nys::AbstractArray{T}: Y coordinates (function values)\n\nReturns\n\nGPRapprox interpolator object that can be called as a function\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.aaad_gpr_pivot"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.aaad_old_reliable-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}}} where T","page":"Other functions","text":"aaad_old_reliable(xs::AbstractArray{T}, ys::AbstractArray{T}) -> AAADapprox\n\nCreates an interpolation function using AAA algorithm from BaryRational.\n\nArguments\n\nxs::AbstractArray{T}: X coordinates\nys::AbstractArray{T}: Y coordinates (function values)\n\nReturns\n\nAAADapprox interpolator object that can be called as a function\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.aaad_old_reliable"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.add_additive_noise-Tuple{OrderedCollections.OrderedDict, Float64}","page":"Other functions","text":"add_additive_noise(data::OrderedDict, noise_level::Float64)\n\nAdd homoskedastic Gaussian noise to each observed series while preserving time points. For each observable, the noise standard deviation is fixed over time: noise_level * mean(abs.(clean_values)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.add_additive_noise"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.add_relative_noise-Tuple{OrderedCollections.OrderedDict, Float64}","page":"Other functions","text":"add_relative_noise(data::OrderedDict, noise_level::Float64)\n\nAdd relative Gaussian noise to data values while preserving time points.\n\nArguments\n\ndata: OrderedDict containing time series data\nnoise_level: Standard deviation of the relative noise to add\n\nReturns\n\nNew OrderedDict with noisy data\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.add_relative_noise"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.agp_gpr_robust-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}}} where T","page":"Other functions","text":"agp_gpr_robust(xs, ys; kernel_type=:se) -> AGPInterpolator\n\nRobust GP interpolator that fixes failures on smooth/noiseless data.\n\nUses softplus reparameterization (hyperparams are always strictly positive), bounded optimization via Fminbox(LBFGS), ForwardDiff exact gradients, and direct Cholesky log-likelihood (avoids ScaledKernel in the optimization loop). Automatically detects smooth data and initializes noise variance low.\n\nFor SE kernel, delegates to _optimize_se_hyperparams (shared with agp_gpr_uq).\n\nReturns an AGPInterpolator compatible with TaylorDiff for derivative computation.\n\nArguments\n\nxs::AbstractArray{T}: Input x-values\nys::AbstractArray{T}: Output y-values\nkernel_type::Symbol: Kernel type: :se (default), :rq, :se_plus_rq or :se_times_rq\n\nReturns\n\nAGPInterpolator: GP interpolator with mean and std prediction functions\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.agp_gpr_robust"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.agp_gpr_uq-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}}} where T","page":"Other functions","text":"agp_gpr_uq(xs::AbstractArray, ys::AbstractArray;\n    kernel_type=:se, lengthscale_factor=1.0) -> AGPInterpolatorUQ\n\nCreate a GP interpolator with full uncertainty quantification support.\n\nLike agp_gpr_robust, but it returns an AGPInterpolatorUQ that stores all the information needed for computing derivative covariances.\n\nArguments\n\nxs::AbstractArray: X coordinates (e.g., time points)\nys::AbstractArray: Y coordinates (observations)\nkernel_type::Symbol: currently only :se (Squared Exponential)\nlengthscale_factor::Float64: opt-in multiplier applied after fitting the function-value marginal-likelihood lengthscale (default: 1.0)\n\nReturns\n\nAGPInterpolatorUQ with full UQ capability\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.agp_gpr_uq"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.analyze_estimation_result-Tuple{ParameterEstimationProblem, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"analyze_estimation_result(problem::ParameterEstimationProblem, result)\n\nAnalyze the results of parameter estimation, including clustering solutions and printing statistics.\n\nArguments\n\nproblem: The parameter estimation problem\nresult: Vector of solution results\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.analyze_estimation_result"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.build_multipoint_template-Tuple{ParameterEstimationProblem, NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"build_multipoint_template(pep_data, setup, si_template; n_points=2, diagnostics=false)\n\nBuild a multi-point polynomial template from the single-point SI template.\n\nAlgorithm\n\nEvaluate SI template at N well-separated probe points\nClassify variables (shared params vs per-point state/data)\nRename per-point variables with _ptK suffix\nCombine and run rank-aware top-down stripping\nBuild the combined SYMBOLIC system (same renaming, no data substitution)\nApply stripping mask to symbolic system\nClassify stripped vars into solvevars vs datavars\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.build_multipoint_template"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.build_noise_frontier_multipoint_template-Tuple{ParameterEstimationProblem, NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"build_noise_frontier_multipoint_template(pep, setup, si_template; kwargs...) -> MultiPointTemplate\n\nBuild a production MultiPointTemplate from the full-equation noise-frontier selection. This is the replacement for build_multipoint_template when system_construction_policy = :noise_frontier.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.build_noise_frontier_multipoint_template"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.build_noise_frontier_system-Tuple{ParameterEstimationProblem, NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"build_noise_frontier_system(pep, setup, si_template; kwargs...) -> NoiseFrontierResult\n\nBuild a noise-first square-system construction frontier. setup must provide good_DD. The default selector is generic/structural and ignores setup.interpolants; pass selection_mode = :instantiated to reproduce the older data-conditioned probe behavior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.build_noise_frontier_system"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.build_observation_covariance","page":"Other functions","text":"build_observation_covariance(gp_results::Dict, times::Vector, max_deriv::Int=2)\n\nBuild the full covariance matrix Σ_z for all observables and derivatives at given times.\n\nIncludes within-observable cross-time covariance blocks from the GP posterior. Cross-observable blocks remain zero (independent GPs for different sensors).\n\nArguments\n\ngp_results::Dict{String, AGPInterpolatorUQ}: GP results keyed by observable name\ntimes::Vector{<:Real}: Time points at which to evaluate\nmax_deriv::Int: Maximum derivative order (default 2)\n\nReturns\n\nμ_z::Vector{Float64}: Full mean vector for all observables and derivatives\nΣ_z::Matrix{Float64}: Full covariance matrix\nlabels::Vector{String}: Labels for each component (e.g., \"y1(t=0.5)\", \"y1'(t=0.5)\")\n\nStructure\n\nThe ordering is: for each observable, for each time, [f, f', f'', ...] So for 2 observables, 3 times, max_deriv=1:   [y1(t1), y1'(t1), y1(t2), y1'(t2), y1(t3), y1'(t3), y2(t1), y2'(t1), ...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.build_observation_covariance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.build_perfect_interpolants-Tuple{ParameterEstimationProblem, Float64, Int64}","page":"Other functions","text":"build_perfect_interpolants(pep, t_eval, max_order; kwargs...)\n\nBuild Dict{Num, PerfectInterpolant} keyed the same way as precomputed_interpolants. Each PerfectInterpolant stores oracle Taylor coefficients and evaluates via Horner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.build_perfect_interpolants"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.calculate_error_stats-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"calculate_error_stats(predicted, actual)\n\nCalculate error statistics between predicted and actual values.\n\nArguments\n\npredicted: Vector of predicted values\nactual: Vector of actual values\n\nReturns\n\nNamed tuple containing absolute and relative error statistics\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.calculate_error_stats"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.calculate_higher_derivative_terms-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Integer}","page":"Other functions","text":"calculate_higher_derivative_terms(lhs_terms, rhs_terms, max_level; independent_variable=t)\n\nCalculate higher derivatives for equally sized arrays of left- and right-hand terms. Input arrays are preserved. Differentiation precedes conversion to jet terms, so higher orders retain the original derivative structure.\n\nArguments\n\nlhs_terms: Initial left-hand terms.\nrhs_terms: Initial right-hand terms.\nmax_level: Nonnegative number of additional derivative levels.\nindependent_variable: Variable with respect to which to differentiate.\n\nReturns\n\nTuple of derivative-level vectors for both sides, beginning at level zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.calculate_higher_derivative_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.calculate_higher_derivatives-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Symbolics.Equation}, Integer}","page":"Other functions","text":"calculate_higher_derivatives(equations, max_level; independent_variable=t)\n\nDifferentiate both sides of each equation through max_level additional orders. Derivative expressions in the returned equations are converted to symbolic jet terms with Symbolics.diff2term.\n\nArguments\n\nequations: Initial symbolic equations.\nmax_level: Nonnegative number of additional derivative levels.\nindependent_variable: Variable with respect to which to differentiate.\n\nReturns\n\nVector of equation vectors, beginning with the initial level in jet form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.calculate_higher_derivatives"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.chebyshev_aicc-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}}} where T","page":"Other functions","text":"chebyshev_aicc(xs, ys) -> ChebyshevApprox\n\nFit a Chebyshev polynomial to data with automatic degree selection via AICc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.chebyshev_aicc"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.chebyshev_bic-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}}} where T","page":"Other functions","text":"chebyshev_bic(xs, ys) -> ChebyshevApprox\n\nFit a Chebyshev polynomial to data with automatic degree selection via BIC. BIC penalizes complexity more than AICc — may prefer lower degree on noisy data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.chebyshev_bic"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.choose_scales-Tuple{ParameterEstimationProblem}","page":"Other functions","text":"choose_scales(pep; objective=:ls, max_abs_exp=60, w_data=1.0, ridge=1e-6) -> ScaleInfo\n\nChoose power-of-2 scale exponents for states/params/observables so the rescaled equations' constants and the data are close to O(1). Builds a small linear system A·θ = b (one row per equation/observable monomial + per directly-observed-state data anchor), solves it (least squares, :ls, via ridge normal equations; or :minmax_irls for a true min-max), and rounds θ to integer exponents. Never errors (degenerate systems return the identity scaling).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.choose_scales"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.clear_denoms-Tuple{Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"clear_denoms(eq)\n\nClear denominators from both sides of an equation containing rational expressions. For example, converts x/y = z to x = y*z.\n\nArguments\n\neq: Equation to process\n\nReturns\n\nModified equation with cleared denominators\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.clear_denoms"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.cluster_solutions-Tuple{Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"cluster_solutions(sorted_results)\n\nGroup similar solutions into clusters based on relative distances.\n\nArguments\n\nsorted_results: Vector of solutions sorted by error\n\nReturns\n\nVector of solution clusters\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.cluster_solutions"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.compatibility_return_code-Tuple{ResultProvenance}","page":"Other functions","text":"compatibility_return_code(provenance::ResultProvenance) -> Symbol\n\nMap canonical provenance metadata onto the legacy return_code compatibility field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.compatibility_return_code"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.compute_observable_taylor_coefficients-Tuple{ParameterEstimationProblem, Dict{Symbolics.Num, Vector{Float64}}, Float64, Int64}","page":"Other functions","text":"compute_observable_taylor_coefficients(pep, state_coeffs, t_eval, max_order)\n\nCompute Taylor coefficients for observables from state Taylor coefficients by walking the observable expression trees.\n\nReturns Dict{Num, Vector{Float64}} keyed by observable RHS expressions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.compute_observable_taylor_coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.compute_oracle_taylor_coefficients-Tuple{ParameterEstimationProblem, Float64, Int64}","page":"Other functions","text":"compute_oracle_taylor_coefficients(pep, t_eval, max_order; kwargs...)\n\nCompute machine-precision Taylor coefficients for all states at t_eval using symbolic RHS recursion (Cauchy products on the expression tree).\n\nReturns state_coeffs::Dict{Num, Vector{Float64}} where each vector has length max_order + 1 with coeffs[k+1] = x^(k)(t_eval) / k!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.compute_oracle_taylor_coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.compute_practical_identifiability_index-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:Real}, Vector{String}, Dict{String, Symbol}, Vector{Float64}}","page":"Other functions","text":"compute_practical_identifiability_index(param_covariance, labels, roles, values; scale_floor=1e-8)\n\nCompute the normalized paper index I_A from propagated parameter covariance. Only physical recovered quantities (parameters and state initial conditions) are included in the default projection; helper derivatives and data variables are excluded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.compute_practical_identifiability_index"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.compute_shooting_indices-Tuple{Int64, Int64}","page":"Other functions","text":"compute_shooting_indices(n_points, n_total; warp=true, beta=3.0) -> Vector{Int}\n\nCompute shooting point indices across a time vector of length n_total.\n\nWhen warp=true and beta > 0, uses an exponential warp to cluster more points near the start of the interval (where transient dynamics are typically richest). When warp=false or beta ≈ 0, returns equidistant indices.\n\nReturns a sorted vector of unique indices in [1, n_total].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.compute_shooting_indices"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.compute_sigma_d-Tuple{Dict{Symbol, <:AbstractDict}, Any, Real, Int64}","page":"Other functions","text":"compute_sigma_d(interpolant_cache, measured_quantities, t_eval, max_order;\n                noise_level=0.0, order_decay_kappa=2.0) -> DerivativeUncertaintyEstimate\n\nBuild a DerivativeUncertaintyEstimate at a single time t_eval.\n\nArguments\n\ninterpolant_cache::Dict{Symbol, <:AbstractDict} — outer keyed by interpolator source symbol (e.g. :aaad, :gpr_pivot), inner keyed by Symbolics RHS of the measured-quantity equations (matches pep.measured_quantities[i].rhs after Symbolics.diff2term if necessary).\nmeasured_quantities — vector of measured-quantity equations (typically pep.measured_quantities); used to look up RHS keys.\nt_eval::Float64 — evaluation time.\nmax_order::Int — highest derivative order to compute σ_d for.\n\nKeyword args\n\nnoise_level::Float64 = 0.0 — measurement noise level (typically opts.noise_level).\norder_decay_kappa::Float64 = 2.0 — multiplicative factor for the order-decay floor. Higher = more pessimistic about higher orders. Empirically reasonable default; not tuned to a specific model.\n\nAlgorithm\n\nFor each (observable, derivative order) cell:\n\nPull nth_deriv(interp, order, t_eval) from each interpolator in the cache that has the observable's RHS as a key. Skip silently if a source can't be evaluated (e.g. the interpolator throws on a boundary).\nCompute σ_empirical = std(values_across_sources) if at least 2 finite values were collected; otherwise 0.0.\nCompute σ_floor = noise_level · order_decay_kappa^order.\nFinal σ_d = max(σ_empirical, σ_floor). Record which one dominated.\n\nReturns a DerivativeUncertaintyEstimate with σd, full provenance of source values used (for diagnostics), and a per-cell `flooractive` flag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.compute_sigma_d"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.construct_equation_system_from_si_template-Tuple{ModelingToolkitBase.AbstractSystem, Vararg{Any, 6}}","page":"Other functions","text":"construct_equation_system_from_si_template(\n\tmodel, measured_quantities, data_sample, deriv_level, unident_dict, varlist, DD;\n\tsi_template, precomputed_interpolants, time_index_set,\n)\n\nBuild the polynomial system at one data point from an equation template.\n\nThe derivatives of each observable at data_sample[\"t\"][time_index_set[1]] are read from precomputed_interpolants and put into si_template.equations. Equations left with no unknowns are dropped. If more equations than unknowns remain, equations are dropped from the end as long as every unknown still appears in one of the rest.\n\nReturns the equations and their unknowns. DD is used only when si_template has no template_DD; model, deriv_level, unident_dict and varlist are not used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.construct_equation_system_from_si_template"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.convert_to_hc_format_with_params-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"convert_to_hc_format_with_params(poly_system, solve_vars, data_vars)\n\nConvert a polynomial system to HomotopyContinuation.System with both variables and parameters.\n\nArguments\n\npoly_system: System of polynomial equations (Symbolics expressions)\nsolve_vars: Variables to solve for (unknowns - parameters and states)\ndata_vars: Variables that become HC parameters (interpolated observable values)\n\nReturns\n\nhc_system: HomotopyContinuation.System with parameters\nhc_variables: HC variable list (corresponding to solve_vars)\nhc_params: HC parameter list (corresponding to data_vars)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.convert_to_hc_format_with_params"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.convert_to_si_ode","page":"Other functions","text":"convert_to_si_ode(ode, measured_quantities::Vector{ModelingToolkit.Equation}, inputs::Vector{Num} = Vector{Num}())\n\nConvert ModelingToolkit ODESystem to StructuralIdentifiability.jl ODE format. Based on ParameterEstimation.jl's preprocess_ode function.\n\nReturns:\n\nsi_ode: StructuralIdentifiability.ODE object\ninput_symbols: Original symbolic variables\ngens: Nemo polynomial ring generators\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.convert_to_si_ode"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.create_interpolants-Tuple{Vector{Symbolics.Equation}, AbstractDict, Vector{Float64}, Function}","page":"Other functions","text":"create_interpolants(\n\tmeasured_quantities::Vector{ModelingToolkit.Equation},\n\tdata_sample::OrderedDict,\n\tt_vector::Vector{Float64},\n\tinterp_func::Function\n) -> Dict{Any, AbstractInterpolator}\n\nCreates interpolant functions for measured quantities using the provided interpolation function.\n\nArguments\n\nmeasured_quantities::Vector{ModelingToolkit.Equation}: Equations for measured quantities\ndata_sample::OrderedDict: Sample data for measured quantities\nt_vector::Vector{Float64}: Time points at which measurements were taken\ninterp_func::Function: Function to use for interpolation (e.g., aaad, aaadgprpivot)\n\nReturns\n\nDictionary mapping each quantity to its corresponding interpolant function\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.create_interpolants"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.create_ordered_ode_system-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"create_ordered_ode_system(name, states, parameters, equations, measured_quantities)\n\nCreate an OrderedODESystem with completed equations and ordered variables.\n\nArguments\n\nname: Name for the system\nstates: State variables\nparameters: System parameters\nequations: System equations\nmeasured_quantities: Equations for measured quantities\n\nReturns\n\nTuple of (OrderedODESystem, measured_quantities)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.create_ordered_ode_system"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.detect_transcendentals-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"detect_transcendentals(equations, measured_quantities, t_var; max_derivative_order=10)\n\nDetect all transcendental subexpressions in the ODE equations and measured quantities.\n\nArguments\n\nequations: Vector of ODE equations (ModelingToolkit Equations)\nmeasured_quantities: Vector of measurement equations\nt_var: The independent variable (time)\nmax_derivative_order: Maximum derivative order to pre-compute (default: 10)\n\nReturns\n\nTranscendentalInfo if transcendentals are found, nothing otherwise\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.detect_transcendentals"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.direct_optimization_parameter_estimation-Tuple{ParameterEstimationProblem}","page":"Other functions","text":"direct_optimization_parameter_estimation(PEP; opts) -> Vector{ParameterEstimationResult}\n\nPerform parameter estimation via direct LBFGS optimization from a random initial guess. Uses the shared PolishContext infrastructure for consistency with the polish path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.direct_optimization_parameter_estimation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.evaluate_data_vars_at_point-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"evaluate_data_vars_at_point(interpolants, data_vars, DD, measured_quantities, t_point)\n\nEvaluate all data variables (observable derivatives) at a specific time point.\n\nArguments\n\ninterpolants: Dict mapping observable RHS to interpolation functions\ndata_vars: Vector of data variables (from extractdatavariablesfromDD)\nDD: DerivativeData structure containing obs_lhs mapping\nmeasured_quantities: Vector of measured quantity equations\nt_point: Time point at which to evaluate\n\nReturns\n\nVector of Float64 values corresponding to data_vars order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.evaluate_data_vars_at_point"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.evaluate_multipoint_template-Tuple{MultiPointTemplate, Vector{Int64}, Dict, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"evaluate_multipoint_template(mpt, time_indices, interpolants, data_sample; diagnostics)\n\nEvaluate the pre-computed template at specific time point indices. Returns a MultiPointEvaluation with data values ready for HC.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.evaluate_multipoint_template"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.evaluate_noise_frontier_data_vars_at_point-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"evaluate_noise_frontier_data_vars_at_point(interpolants, data_vars, measured_quantities, t_point)\n\nEvaluate the selected frontier candidate's data variables in the exact order expected by HC parameter homotopy. Data variables are clean SIAN-style names (y1_0, y2_3, possibly suffixed with _ptK) plus optional _trfn_ variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.evaluate_noise_frontier_data_vars_at_point"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.extract_data_variables_from_DD-Tuple{Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"extract_data_variables_from_DD(DD::DerivativeData)\n\nExtract all observable derivative variables from the DerivativeData structure. These are the variables that should become HC parameters in parameter homotopy.\n\nReturns\n\nVector of Symbolics variables representing y_i^(j) for all observables and derivative levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.extract_data_variables_from_DD"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.generate_sensitivity_seeds-Tuple{Vector, AbstractMatrix{<:Real}, Vector{String}, Vector{String}, DerivativeUncertaintyEstimate, Any, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"generate_sensitivity_seeds(candidates, S, sigma_d_estimate, polish_ctx, measured_quantities;\n                           probe_scale=1.0, eigenvalue_threshold=0.01,\n                           mahalanobis_threshold=-1.0)\n    -> (Vector{Vector{Float64}}, SensitivitySeedReport)\n\nGenerate additional polish seeds from a candidate pool using a reference sensitivity matrix S and per-derivative uncertainty sigma_d_estimate.\n\nArguments\n\ncandidates::Vector{ParameterEstimationResult} — pool from the polynomial pipeline (post-cluster, pre-polish).\nS::Matrix{Float64} — reference data-sensitivity matrix from _compute_data_sensitivity. Shape (n_unknowns, n_data).\ndata_labels::Vector{String} — column labels of S.\nunknown_labels::Vector{String} — row labels of S. Used to align with the candidate's parameter ordering via polish_ctx.unknown_syms/param_syms.\nsigma_d_estimate::DerivativeUncertaintyEstimate — at the reference t_eval.\npolish_ctx::PolishContext — for converting candidates to parameter vectors.\nmeasured_quantities — for _build_obs_name_to_idx.\n\nKeywords\n\nprobe_scale::Float64 = 1.0 — multiplier on √λ for ±σ probes.\neigenvalue_threshold::Float64 = 0.01 — only emit probes for eigenvalues with λ_i / λ_max > eigenvalue_threshold.\nmahalanobis_threshold::Float64 = -1.0 — Mahalanobis-d² cutoff for blends. Negative means \"use chi² 95% quantile for n_unknowns DoF.\"\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple (seeds, report):\n\nseeds::Vector{Vector{Float64}} — the emitted polish seeds in external (user-space) coordinates, one per row, one row per seed.\nreport::SensitivitySeedReport — counts and diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.generate_sensitivity_seeds"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.get_ad_backend-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Other functions","text":"get_ad_backend(backend::Symbol)\n\nConvert AD backend symbol to an Optimization.jl AD type.\n\nSupported backends\n\n:forward → AutoForwardDiff() (default, works with most problems)\n:finite → AutoFiniteDiff() (fallback, no AD required)\n\nNote: :zygote and :enzyme were removed. Zygote segfaults Julia 1.12's JIT compiler, and Enzyme cannot compile the trajectory loss, which rebuilds the ODE problem through symbolic indexing. ForwardDiff is the recommended backend (the only AD backend that works through adaptive ODE solvers).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.get_ad_backend"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.get_interpolator_function","page":"Other functions","text":"get_interpolator_function(method::InterpolatorMethod, custom::Union{Nothing, Function}=nothing; s3_adapt_k=10.0) -> Function\n\nConvert InterpolatorMethod enum to actual interpolator function. The s3_adapt_k keyword controls the noise multiplier for S3 adaptive-tolerance methods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.get_interpolator_function"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.get_polish_optimizer-Tuple{PolishMethod}","page":"Other functions","text":"get_polish_optimizer(method::PolishMethod)\n\nConvert PolishMethod enum to actual optimizer object/type.\n\nFor scalar Optimization.solve-based methods (legacy default PolishNewtonTrust, plus PolishBFGS/PolishLBFGS) returns a zero-arg constructor — the call site does optimizer_type() to instantiate.\n\nFor residual-mode methods (PolishLSOBoundedLog, PolishFastLMBoundedLog) returns a tagged tuple (kind::Symbol, factory) consumed by _polish_single_residual. The kinds are :lso_direct (LeastSquaresOptim) and :fastlm_direct (FastLevenbergMarquardt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.get_polish_optimizer"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.get_si_equation_system-Tuple{Any, Vector{Symbolics.Equation}, AbstractDict}","page":"Other functions","text":"get_si_equation_system(ode, measured_quantities::Vector{ModelingToolkit.Equation}, data_sample::OrderedDict; DD=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nGet polynomial equation system from StructuralIdentifiability.jl. This replaces the iterative equation construction in ODEPE.\n\nReturns:\n\nequations: Polynomial equations in Symbolics format (template)\nderivative_vars: Dictionary mapping derivative variables to their orders\nunidentifiable: Set of unidentifiable parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.get_si_equation_system"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.get_sigma_d-Tuple{DerivativeUncertaintyEstimate, Int64, Int64}","page":"Other functions","text":"get_sigma_d(estimate, obs_idx, order) -> Float64\n\nPer-(observable, order) lookup. Returns NaN if the request is out of range.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.get_sigma_d"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.get_solver_function-Tuple{SystemSolverMethod}","page":"Other functions","text":"get_solver_function(method::SystemSolverMethod) -> Function\n\nConvert SystemSolverMethod enum to actual solver function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.get_solver_function"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.gp_derivative_influence_matrix","page":"Other functions","text":"gp_derivative_influence_matrix(interp, t, max_deriv) -> (mean, W)\n\nFor fixed GP hyperparameters, compute the linear map from raw observations y to the posterior-mean jet [f(t), f'(t), ...].\n\nThe current GP implementation centers and scales y before fitting.  This routine folds the centering operation into W, so W * raw_y reproduces the implemented derivative estimator exactly up to floating-point roundoff.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.gp_derivative_influence_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.gp_factorization_diagnostics-Tuple{AGPInterpolatorUQ}","page":"Other functions","text":"gp_factorization_diagnostics(interp::AGPInterpolatorUQ)\n\nReturn scale-aware numerical telemetry for the GP factorization used by both the estimator mean and derivative UQ. :material_regularization means the added Cholesky jitter exceeded the learned observation-noise variance. This is a warning axis, not by itself proof that the retained linear solve is invalid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.gp_factorization_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.hmcs-Tuple{Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"hmcs(x)\n\nConvert a string to a HomotopyContinuation ModelKit Variable. Helper function used when converting symbolic expressions to HC format.\n\nArguments\n\nx: String to convert\n\nReturns\n\nHomotopyContinuation ModelKit Variable\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.hmcs"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.interpolator_method_to_symbol-Tuple{InterpolatorMethod}","page":"Other functions","text":"interpolator_method_to_symbol(method::InterpolatorMethod) -> Symbol\n\nConvert an InterpolatorMethod enum value to a Symbol for tagging results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.interpolator_method_to_symbol"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.joint_derivative_covariance","page":"Other functions","text":"joint_derivative_covariance(interp::AGPInterpolatorUQ, t::Real, max_deriv::Int=2)\n\nCompute the joint posterior covariance matrix for [f(t), f'(t), ..., f^(max_deriv)(t)].\n\nThis is the key function for UQ: it gives us the covariance structure of the observable and its derivatives at a single time point.\n\nArguments\n\ninterp::AGPInterpolatorUQ: The GP interpolator with stored hyperparameters\nt::Real: Time point at which to compute covariance\nmax_deriv::Int: Maximum derivative order to include (default 2)\n\nReturns\n\nμ::Vector{Float64}: Posterior means [f(t), f'(t), ..., f^(max_deriv)(t)]\nΣ::Matrix{Float64}: Posterior covariance matrix\n\nTheory\n\nThe posterior covariance is:     Σposterior = Σprior - K*n K⁻¹ Kn*\n\nwhere:\n\nΣ_prior is the prior covariance matrix for derivatives at t\nK_*n is the covariance between test derivatives and training points\nK⁻¹ is the inverse of the training covariance (via Cholesky)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.joint_derivative_covariance"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.joint_derivative_estimator_covariance","page":"Other functions","text":"joint_derivative_estimator_covariance(interp, t, max_deriv=2; kwargs...)\n\nCompute the sampling covariance of the GP posterior-mean derivative estimator. More precisely: the repeated-observation sampling covariance evaluated at plug-in (fitted) hyperparameters — how the jet estimates would fluctuate if the observations were redrawn around a fixed underlying signal with noise covariance Σ_y. (Hyperparameter-estimation variability is omitted; the direction and magnitude of the resulting covariance error are not guaranteed.)\n\nThis is intentionally different from joint_derivative_covariance, which returns the latent GP posterior covariance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.joint_derivative_estimator_covariance"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.joint_derivative_estimator_covariance-2","page":"Other functions","text":"joint_derivative_estimator_covariance(interp, ts::AbstractVector, max_deriv=2; kwargs...)\n\nStacked multi-time estimator-sampling covariance of one observable's jets: Σ = W_stack · Σ_y · W_stackᵀ with W_stack = vcat(W(t₁), …, W(t_T)). Cross-time blocks W(tᵢ) Σ_y W(tⱼ)ᵀ are nonzero because every jet is a linear functional of the SAME training observations — this is the repeated-observation sampling covariance at plug-in hyperparameters, NOT the latent GP posterior cross-time covariance (build_observation_covariance computes that distinct object; the two must never be mixed).\n\nFactorized construction is PSD up to roundoff whenever Σy is PSD. Tiny negative eigenvalues are clipped to zero; materially negative ones warn loudly (construction-bug indicator). Genuine singularity — e.g. repeated evaluation times — is preserved, never shifted away: `S Σd Sᵀ` downstream needs no inverse of this matrix.\n\nRow order is time-major (per time, orders 0:max_deriv); map indices with stacked_jet_index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.joint_derivative_estimator_covariance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.learned_observation_noise_variance-Tuple{AGPInterpolatorUQ}","page":"Other functions","text":"learned_observation_noise_variance(interp::AGPInterpolatorUQ) -> Float64\n\nReturn the GP-learned observation noise variance in the original observation units.  AGPInterpolatorUQ.noise_var is learned after z-scoring the observable, so it must be multiplied by y_std^2 before it is used with raw-data influence weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.learned_observation_noise_variance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.lineage_summary-Tuple{Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"lineage_summary(result) -> String\n\nCompact human-readable summary of result provenance for logs and diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.lineage_summary"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.mean_and_var-Tuple{AGPInterpolator, Real}","page":"Other functions","text":"mean_and_var(interp::AGPInterpolator, x::Real) -> (Float64, Float64)\n\nReturns both mean prediction and variance at point x.\n\nArguments\n\ninterp::AGPInterpolator: The GP interpolator\nx::Real: Point at which to evaluate\n\nReturns\n\nTuple of (mean, variance) - both denormalized to original data scale\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.mean_and_var"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.merge_options-Tuple{EstimationOptions}","page":"Other functions","text":"merge_options(base::EstimationOptions; kwargs...) -> EstimationOptions\n\nCreate a new EstimationOptions struct by merging keyword arguments with an existing options struct. This is useful for temporarily overriding specific options.\n\nExamples\n\nbase_opts = EstimationOptions()\nnew_opts = merge_options(base_opts; abstol=1e-10, debug_solver=true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.merge_options"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.nth_deriv-Tuple{AGPInterpolatorUQ, Int64, Real}","page":"Other functions","text":"nth_deriv(f::AGPInterpolatorUQ, n::Int, t::Real) -> Real\n\nAnalytic nth derivative of the GP posterior mean. Uses the closed-form SE kernel derivative formula via _build_K_star_n — avoids TaylorDiff and is exact.\n\nThe GP posterior mean is: μ(t) = ystd * Kn(t) · α + ymean Its nth derivative is:  μ^(n)(t) = ystd * K_n[n+1, :] · α\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.nth_deriv"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.nth_deriv-Tuple{Function, Int64, Real}","page":"Other functions","text":"nth_deriv(f::Function, n::Int, t::Real) -> Real\n\nComputes the nth derivative of function f at point t using TaylorDiff.\n\nArguments\n\nf::Function: Function to differentiate\nn::Int: Derivative order\nt::Real: Point at which to compute the derivative\n\nReturns\n\nValue of the nth derivative of f at t\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.nth_deriv"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.optimized_multishot_parameter_estimation","page":"Other functions","text":"optimized_multishot_parameter_estimation(PEP; kwargs...)\n\nOptimized parameter estimation using precomputed derivatives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.optimized_multishot_parameter_estimation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.print_options-Tuple{IO, EstimationOptions}","page":"Other functions","text":"print_options(io::IO, opts::EstimationOptions; compact=false)\n\nPretty-print the options struct.\n\nArguments\n\nio::IO: Output stream\nopts::EstimationOptions: Options to print\ncompact::Bool: If true, only print non-default values\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.print_options"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.print_uncertainty_results-Tuple{UncertaintyReport}","page":"Other functions","text":"print_uncertainty_results(uq::UncertaintyReport; io = stdout)\n\nTerminal summary of the parameter uncertainty report. Public reports are in physical coordinates [parameters, initial_conditions]; local IFT coordinates are printed separately when available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.print_uncertainty_results"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.provenance_metadata_dict-Tuple{ResultProvenance}","page":"Other functions","text":"provenance_metadata_dict(prov::ResultProvenance) -> OrderedDict{String, Any}\n\nSingle source of the provenance block written into odepe_metadata.json by the PEB / experiments benchmark templates. This block used to be copy-pasted into ~8 template writers across two repos, which produced stragglers on every provenance-schema change (2026-08-12 dedup). Values are JSON-friendly (Symbols stringified, OrderedDicts re-keyed by string). Template-local extras (parameters, states, all_unidentifiable, …) are merged on top by the caller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.provenance_metadata_dict"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.rescale_pep-Tuple{ParameterEstimationProblem}","page":"Other functions","text":"rescale_pep(pep) -> (rescaled_pep, ScaleInfo) or (pep, nothing)\n\nApply power-of-2 rescaling. Returns (pep, nothing) unchanged when scaling is trivial (all exponents 0) — an idempotent no-op, like the transcendental wrapper.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.rescale_pep"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.resolve_interpolator_list-Tuple{EstimationOptions}","page":"Other functions","text":"resolve_interpolator_list(opts::EstimationOptions) -> Vector{Tuple{InterpolatorMethod, Union{Nothing, Function}}}\n\nThe interpolators to run, as (method, custom_func_or_nothing) tuples, in the order of opts.interpolators. Each InterpolatorCustom entry takes the next function in opts.custom_interpolators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.resolve_interpolator_list"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.s3_refine_gp-Tuple{AbstractInterpolator, Vector{Float64}, Vector{Float64}}","page":"Other functions","text":"s3_refine_gp(gp_interpolant::AbstractInterpolator, t_vector, y_raw;\n             aaa_tol, mmax, maxiter, g_tol) -> AAADapprox\n\nS3 strategy: take a GP interpolant, evaluate it at data points to get denoised values, run AAA on the denoised values (tight tolerance for more support points), then MLE-refine the barycentric weights against the RAW noisy data.\n\nReturns an AAADapprox whose call method uses baryEval (AD-friendly at support points).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.s3_refine_gp"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.s3_refine_gp_adaptive-Tuple{AbstractInterpolator, Vector{Float64}, Vector{Float64}}","page":"Other functions","text":"s3_refine_gp_adaptive(gp_interpolant, t_vector, y_raw; k, mmax, maxiter, g_tol) -> AAADapprox\n\nAdaptive-tolerance S3 strategy: estimate noise from GP residuals, then set aaa_tol = max(k * noise_std / data_scale, 1e-14).  Higher k → fewer support points (more aggressive denoising).  k=10 is the validated default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.s3_refine_gp_adaptive"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.s3_refine_gp_bic-Tuple{AbstractInterpolator, Vector{Float64}, Vector{Float64}}","page":"Other functions","text":"s3_refine_gp_bic(gp_interpolant, t_vector, y_raw; mmax, maxiter, g_tol) -> AAADapprox\n\nBIC model-selection S3 strategy: sweep 12 AAA tolerances (1e-3 … 1e-14), pick the one minimising BIC = n_params * log(M) + M * log(SSR / M) against the raw data.  Parameter-free alternative to adaptive tolerance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.s3_refine_gp_bic"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.se_kernel_cross_time_covariance_matrix","page":"Other functions","text":"se_kernel_cross_time_covariance_matrix(σ²::Real, ℓ::Real, delta_t::Real, max_deriv::Int=2) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nCompute the prior covariance matrix between derivative vectors at two time points separated by delta_t.\n\nEntry [a+1, b+1] = Cov(f^(a)(t), f^(b)(t')) where t - t' = delta_t.\n\nReduces to se_kernel_prior_covariance_matrix when delta_t == 0.\n\nArguments\n\nσ²::Real: Signal variance\nℓ::Real: Lengthscale\ndelta_t::Real: Time difference t - t'\nmax_deriv::Int: Maximum derivative order (default 2)\n\nReturns\n\nMatrix of size (maxderiv+1) × (maxderiv+1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.se_kernel_cross_time_covariance_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.se_kernel_derivative-Tuple{Real, Real, Real, Int64, Int64}","page":"Other functions","text":"se_kernel_derivative(σ²::Real, ℓ::Real, Δt::Real, i::Int, j::Int) -> Real\n\nCompute the SE kernel derivative: ∂^(i+j)/∂t^i∂t'^j k(t, t') evaluated at Δt = t - t'.\n\nThe SE kernel is k(Δt) = σ² exp(-Δt²/(2ℓ²)).\n\nUses closed-form expressions derived from the product rule and the fact that ∂ⁿ/∂xⁿ exp(-x²/2) = Hₙ(x) exp(-x²/2) where Hₙ is the probabilist's Hermite polynomial.\n\nArguments\n\nσ²::Real: Signal variance\nℓ::Real: Lengthscale\nΔt::Real: Time difference t - t'\ni::Int: Order of derivative with respect to t (0 ≤ i ≤ 4)\nj::Int: Order of derivative with respect to t' (0 ≤ j ≤ 4)\n\nReturns\n\nThe value of the kernel derivative at Δt\n\nExamples\n\n# Prior variance of f(t)\nse_kernel_derivative(1.0, 0.5, 0.0, 0, 0)  # = σ² = 1.0\n\n# Prior variance of f'(t)\nse_kernel_derivative(1.0, 0.5, 0.0, 1, 1)  # = σ²/ℓ² = 4.0\n\n# Covariance between f(t) and f''(t) at same point\nse_kernel_derivative(1.0, 0.5, 0.0, 0, 2)  # = -σ²/ℓ² = -4.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.se_kernel_derivative"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.se_kernel_prior_covariance_matrix","page":"Other functions","text":"se_kernel_prior_covariance_matrix(σ²::Real, ℓ::Real, max_deriv::Int=2) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nCompute the prior covariance matrix for [f(t), f'(t), f''(t), ...] at a single point t.\n\nFor the SE kernel at t = t' (Δt = 0), many cross-covariances are zero due to symmetry:\n\nCov(f, f') = 0 (odd symmetry)\nCov(f', f'') = 0 (odd symmetry)\n\nArguments\n\nσ²::Real: Signal variance\nℓ::Real: Lengthscale\nmax_deriv::Int: Maximum derivative order (default 2)\n\nReturns\n\nSymmetric matrix of size (maxderiv+1) × (maxderiv+1)\n\nExample\n\nFor SE kernel with σ²=1, ℓ=1, the matrix for [f, f', f''] is:\n\n[  1      0    -1   ]\n[  0      1     0   ]\n[ -1      0     3   ]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.se_kernel_prior_covariance_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.seed_vectors_to_candidates-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Real}}, Any, Real}","page":"Other functions","text":"seed_vectors_to_candidates(seeds, polish_ctx, t0; source_type=:sensitivity_seed)\n    -> Vector{ParameterEstimationResult}\n\nWrap each Vector{Float64} seed (in [states; params] order matching polish_ctx.unknown_syms ++ polish_ctx.param_syms) into a synthetic ParameterEstimationResult with err = nothing and provenance tagged source_type=:sensitivity_seed. The polish phase will compute the actual err post-polish via set_result_lineage!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.seed_vectors_to_candidates"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.solve_multipoint_direct-Tuple{MultiPointEvaluation}","page":"Other functions","text":"solve_multipoint_direct(eval; options)\n\nDirect solve: substitute data values into symbolic equations, then solve with HC.jl. Returns a vector of real solution vectors (each in solve_vars order).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.solve_multipoint_direct"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.solve_multipoint_overdetermined-Tuple{MultiPointTemplate, MultiPointEvaluation, Vector, Vector}","page":"Other functions","text":"solve_multipoint_overdetermined(mpt, eval_result, all_equations, all_vars;\n    diagnostics=false)\n\nSolve using HC on the stripped (square) system, then refine each solution against the full overdetermined (pre-strip) system using Levenberg-Marquardt.\n\nReturns refined solution vectors in mpt.solve_vars order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.solve_multipoint_overdetermined"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.solve_multipoint_parameterized-Tuple{MultiPointTemplate, Vector{MultiPointEvaluation}}","page":"Other functions","text":"solve_multipoint_parameterized(mpt, evaluations; options)\n\nParameter homotopy solve: datavars are HC parameters, solvevars are HC variables. Solves at first evaluation using fresh polyhedral solve, then tracks solutions to subsequent evaluations.\n\nReturns one vector of solutions per evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.solve_multipoint_parameterized"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.solve_with_fast_nlopt-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"solve_with_fast_nlopt(poly_system, varlist; kwargs...)\n\nLevenberg–Marquardt on the polynomial system from one starting point (random unless start_point is given), with a ForwardDiff Jacobian. The system is compiled with Symbolics.build_function, falling back to substitution if that fails. If the iteration limit is reached it retries once with NonlinearSolve's FastShortcutNLLSPolyalg. A point is returned only when the solver reports success; otherwise the solution list is empty. It minimizes the sum of squared residuals, so for a system with no solution it can return that minimum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.solve_with_fast_nlopt"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.solve_with_hc-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"solve_with_hc(poly_system, varlist; options=Dict())\n\nSolve a square polynomial system using HomotopyContinuation.jl. Returns the same tuple layout as other solvers: (solutions, hcvarlist, trivialdict, trimmedvarlist). Solutions are vectors of Float64 in the order of varlist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.solve_with_hc"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.solve_with_hc_parameterized-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"solve_with_hc_parameterized(poly_system, solve_vars, data_vars, param_values_list; options=Dict())\n\nSolve a polynomial system at multiple parameter values using parameter homotopy.\n\nThis function provides significant speedup when solving the same polynomial structure at multiple shooting points, as it tracks existing solutions instead of computing fresh start systems for each point.\n\nArguments\n\npoly_system: Symbolic equations (structure fixed, coefficients vary)\nsolve_vars: Variables to solve for (parameters and states)\ndata_vars: Variables that become HC parameters (interpolated observables like y1(t), y1'(t))\nparam_values_list: Vector of parameter value vectors, one per shooting point\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\noptions::Dict: Solver options (e.g., :showprogress, :realtol)\n\nReturns\n\nVector of vectors of real solutions, one per shooting point.\n\nAlgorithm\n\nFirst point: Fresh solve with HC.solve(), collect ALL solutions (real + complex)\nSubsequent points: Parameter homotopy tracking ALL solutions from previous point\nAt each point: Filter to real solutions for output\nTrack ALL solutions to next point (solutions can transition between real/complex)\n\nFallback\n\nIf parameter homotopy tracking loses solutions, falls back to fresh solve at that point and emits a warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.solve_with_hc_parameterized"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.solve_with_robust-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"solve_with_robust(poly_system, varlist; kwargs...)\n\nA robust polynomial system solver that uses the best algorithms from our benchmarking. Supports both solving from scratch and polishing mode.\n\nIt is selected with system_solver = SolverRobust, and the estimation also uses it with polish_only = true to refine the roots homotopy continuation finds.\n\nArguments\n\npoly_system: Vector of polynomial equations (Symbolics expressions)\nvarlist: Vector of variables (Symbolics variables)\n\nKeywords\n\nstart_point=nothing: Initial guess. If nothing, uses random or multistart\npolish_only=false: If true, only does quick local refinement\nprepared_system=nothing: Optional reusable kernel from prepare_robust_system.\ndata_values=Float64[]: Numerical data in the prepared kernel's declared order.\noptions=Dict(): Additional options including:\n:debug => true/false: Print debug information\n:jacobian => :forwarddiff/:symbolic/:finitediff/:none: Jacobian method (default :forwarddiff for an unprepared call; otherwise the prepared kernel's method)\n:forwarddiff_chunk_size => 1: AD directions per chunk; zero selects automatically\n:abstol => 1e-8: Absolute tolerance\n:reltol => 1e-6: Relative tolerance\n:maxiters => 1000: Maximum iterations\n:algorithm => :auto/:trustregion/:bfgs/:bobyqa/:levenberg: Force specific algorithm\n:multistart => true/false: Use multiple starting points\n:timeout => 300.0: Wall-clock budget in seconds. Enforced between starting points AND per solver call (NonlinearSolve maxtime, checked per iteration — a single pathological iteration can still overrun; the deadline-Ref-in-residual pattern from polish_residual.jl:22-28 is the escalation lever if that bites)\n\nReturns\n\n(solutions, varlist, Dict(), varlist), the format of the other system solvers, where solutions is a vector of solution vectors in varlist order (empty if no start converged).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.solve_with_robust"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.stacked_jet_index-Tuple{StackedJetInfluenceEstimate, Int64, Int64}","page":"Other functions","text":"stacked_jet_index(est::StackedJetInfluenceEstimate, time_idx, order) -> Int\n\nRow/column index of (time time_idx, derivative order) in the time-major stacked jet ordering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.stacked_jet_index"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.sync_result_contract!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"sync_result_contract!(result) -> result\n\nSynchronize compatibility fields that are now derived from provenance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.sync_result_contract!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.tag_symbol-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"tag_symbol(symbol, prefix, suffix)\n\nAdd prefix and suffix tags to a symbol, handling special cases like time-dependent variables. For example: tagsymbol(x(t), \"pre\", \"post\") -> prextpost\n\nArguments\n\nsymbol: Symbol to modify\nprefix: String to add before the symbol\nsuffix: String to add after the symbol\n\nReturns\n\nNew tagged symbolic variable\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.tag_symbol"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.transform_pep_for_estimation-Tuple{ParameterEstimationProblem, Any}","page":"Other functions","text":"transform_pep_for_estimation(pep::ParameterEstimationProblem, t_var)\n\nTransform a ParameterEstimationProblem that contains transcendental functions into an equivalent polynomial problem. This is the main entry point for transcendental handling.\n\nArguments\n\npep::ParameterEstimationProblem: Original problem (may contain sin/cos/exp)\nt_var: Independent variable\n\nReturns\n\n(new_pep, tr_info): Transformed problem and transcendental info, or (pep, nothing) if no transformation needed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.transform_pep_for_estimation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.unpack_ODE-Tuple{ModelingToolkitBase.AbstractSystem}","page":"Other functions","text":"unpack_ODE(model::ModelingToolkit.AbstractSystem)\n\nExtract the core components of an ODESystem.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::ModelingToolkit.AbstractSystem: The ODE system to unpack\n\nReturns\n\nTuple containing (independent variable, equations, state variables, parameters)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.unpack_ODE"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.unrescale_results-Tuple{Any, ScaleInfo}","page":"Other functions","text":"unrescale_results(solved_res, info::ScaleInfo)\n\nUn-rescale a vector of ParameterEstimationResult IN PLACE: parameters[p] *= 2^{b_j}, states[x] *= 2^{a_i}. .err is LEFT in scaled-observable units (it is a collapsed multi-observable sum and not invertible by a single factor); a :rescaled provenance note is stamped. .solution is left in scaled coordinates (documented limitation; consumers read .parameters/.states).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.unrescale_results"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.unrescale_uncertainty_report-Tuple{Union{Nothing, AbstractUQOutcome}, ScaleInfo}","page":"Other functions","text":"unrescale_uncertainty_report(uq, info) -> UncertaintyReport\n\nConvert a UQ report produced inside the automatic power-of-two rescaled problem back to original parameter/state units. The estimation pipeline computes UQ before unrescale_results, because the solver trajectory and local algebraic coordinates are still in the scaled problem at that point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.unrescale_uncertainty_report"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.uq_metadata_dict-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"Other functions","text":"JSON-friendly additive sidecar block for a requested UQ outcome.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.uq_metadata_dict"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.uq_reliability-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"Other functions","text":"uq_reliability(outcome) -> UQReliabilityAssessment\n\nReturn the independent reliability axes for a requested UQ outcome.  A single run can establish availability and pass local numerical gates, but it cannot certify repeated-sampling coverage; empirical calibration therefore remains :not_assessed_by_single_run for every individual report.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.uq_reliability"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.validate_options-Tuple{EstimationOptions}","page":"Other functions","text":"validate_options(opts::EstimationOptions) -> Bool\n\nValidate that the options struct has sensible values. Throws warnings for potentially problematic configurations.\n\nReturns\n\ntrue if options are valid\nfalse if there are critical issues\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.validate_options"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/functions/#ODEParameterEstimation.with_estimation_timing-Tuple{Function}","page":"Other functions","text":"with_estimation_timing(f) -> (value, timing)\n\nRun an estimation workflow while capturing the package's structured timing breakdown, even when profile_phases=false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.with_estimation_timing"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Contributing","page":"Contributing","text":"Bug reports and pull requests are welcome on GitHub.","title":"Contributing"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Running-the-tests","page":"Contributing","text":"Add your checkout with Pkg.develop, then run these from Julia's default environment:\n\njulia --startup-file=no test/current.jl unit        # quick tests of the parts\njulia --startup-file=no test/current.jl             # everything\njulia --startup-file=no test/current.jl benchmark   # recovery from noisy data on full-size models\njulia --startup-file=no test/current.jl examples    # estimates the example models\n\nRun the full suite for any change that touches estimation. unit is for quick iteration and does not replace it.\n\nexamples estimates each model in ODEParameterEstimation.Examples with default options and checks the answer against test/example_expectations.jl. It takes about twenty minutes. A few models take many minutes each; those are in a group of their own, examples_slow, which estimates each model in a Julia process of its own. Both run weekly. Every push runs examples_ci, five models chosen in that file. A new example model needs an entry there.\n\nTo check that the package installs and passes with dependencies taken from the registry only, with none of your local development versions:\n\njulia --startup-file=no test/registered.jl","title":"Running the tests"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Precompiling","page":"Contributing","text":"While the package is precompiled it estimates a small model, so that most of what estimate needs is compiled before anyone calls it. With that step, precompiling takes about six minutes every time src changes. Without it, it takes half a minute. To skip it while you work, put this in a LocalPreferences.toml beside the Project.toml of the environment you develop in:\n\n[ODEParameterEstimation]\nprecompile_workload = false\n\nJulia also reads that file from your default environment when another environment is active, so a setting left there reaches every environment that does not set its own.\n\nTake it out again before you time anything or run the tests for the last time. While it is there, they run on a package precompiled without that step.","title":"Precompiling"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Building-this-manual","page":"Contributing","text":"julia --startup-file=no --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --startup-file=no --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nThe pages are in docs/src. Their examples run during the build, so a page that builds is a page whose code works. Open docs/build/index.html to read the result.","title":"Building this manual"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Engineering-notes","page":"Contributing","text":"docs/internal holds the notes written while the package was developed: investigations, design discussions and release checklists. They are a record, not documentation, and many describe code that has since changed. Its review_map.md says where things are in the source.","title":"Engineering notes"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/identifiability/#More-than-one-answer","page":"More than one answer","text":"For some models the data cannot single out one set of parameter values. That is a property of the model and of what was measured. It is not a failure of the fit, and more data or better data does not change it. There are two ways it happens, and the package reports both.","title":"More than one answer"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/identifiability/#Several-separate-answers","page":"More than one answer","text":"Two substances decay at their own rates, and only their total is measured:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) y(t)\n@named decays = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x1, D(x2) ~ -b * x2], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(decays, [y ~ x1 + x2];\n    true_values = [a => 0.5, b => 1.5, x1 => 2.0, x2 => 1.0])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 41, time_interval = [0.0, 3.0])\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\n2-element Vector{ParameterEstimationResult}:\n ParameterEstimationResult(a = 1.5, b = 0.5; x1(t) = 1, x2(t) = 2)\n ParameterEstimationResult(a = 0.5, b = 1.5; x1(t) = 2, x2(t) = 1)\n\nThere are two results, and they are the same answer with the substances swapped:\n\nresults[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 1.5\n    b = 0.5\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 1\n    x2(t) = 2\n  Fit error: 3.06e-27\n\nresults[2]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.5\n    b = 1.5\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 2\n    x2(t) = 1\n  Fit error: 3.53e-27\n\nBoth reproduce the total exactly, and nothing in the data says which substance is which. If you know something more, for instance that the first one decays more slowly, that picks the answer. The package cannot know it, so it gives you every answer the data allow.","title":"Several separate answers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/identifiability/#Parameters-that-cannot-be-determined","page":"More than one answer","text":"In this model two rates only ever appear as a sum, and the third state is never measured:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b c d\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) x3(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named chain = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x1, D(x2) ~ (b + c) * x1, D(x3) ~ d * x1], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(chain, [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x2];\n    true_values = [a => 0.1, b => 0.2, c => 0.3, d => 0.4, x1 => 2.0, x2 => 3.0, x3 => 4.0])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 41, time_interval = [0.0, 5.0])\n\nbest = estimate(problem)[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.1\n    c = 4  (not identifiable)\n    b = -3.5  (not identifiable)\n    d = 0.5  (not identifiable)\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 2\n    x2(t) = 3\n    x3(t) = 0.60708  (not identifiable)\n  Fit error: 1.16e-26\n\nThe data determine a, the two measured states, and the sum b + c:\n\nbest[b] + best[c]\n0.49999999999998757\n\nThey say nothing about how that sum splits into b and c, and nothing about d or x3. Those are marked not identifiable. To solve for everything else the package fixes each of them at some value, and that value is what is printed. It is arbitrary. Do not read anything into it.\n\nThe same information is available as a set:\n\nbest.all_unidentifiable\nSet{Symbolics.Num} with 4 elements:\n  d\n  c\n  b\n  x3(t)\n\nThere are two ways out. One is to measure more: with x3 measured as well, d can be found. The other is to say less: write the model with a single parameter in place of b + c, and it has one answer.\n\nStructuralIdentifiability.jl answers these questions for a model before any data exist. This package runs it for you at the start of every estimation.","title":"Parameters that cannot be determined"},{"category":"section","location":"#ODEParameterEstimation.jl","page":"Home","text":"ODEParameterEstimation.jl fits the parameters and initial conditions of an ODE model to time-series data. You give it the model and the measurements. It returns every parameter set that fits them, and tells you which parameters the data cannot determine.\n\nIt works by solving equations rather than by searching. Derivatives of the data are estimated, the model turns them into polynomial equations for the unknowns, and those equations are solved for all their solutions, which are then refined against the data. Because the equations must be polynomial, the model has to be built from polynomials and ratios of polynomials. That covers mass-action kinetics, population and epidemic models, compartment models and most linear systems.\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters α β γ δ\n@variables prey(t) predators(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named lotka_volterra = System([\n    D(prey) ~ α * prey - β * prey * predators,\n    D(predators) ~ δ * prey * predators - γ * predators,\n], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(lotka_volterra, [y1 ~ prey, y2 ~ predators];\n    data = (t = times, y1 = prey_counts, y2 = predator_counts))\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\n\nGetting started runs this example and explains each line.","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Finding-your-way","page":"Home","text":"Tutorials each work through one situation: your own data, noisy data, models with more than one answer and uncertainty, which is experimental.\nGuides cover one topic each: which models work, the options worth knowing, how to read results, how the method works, and what to try when something goes wrong.\nReference documents the main functions and types.","title":"Finding your way"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing","page":"Home","text":"If you use this package in your work, please cite the papers it is based on:\n\nO. Bassik, A. Demin, A. Ovchinnikov. Practical algebraic parameter estimation for noisy data via Gaussian process regression. arXiv:2609.30451 (2026).\n\nO. Bassik, Y. Berman, S. Go, H. Hong, I. Ilmer, A. Ovchinnikov, C. Rackauckas, P. Soto, C. Yap. Robust parameter estimation for rational ordinary differential equations. Applied Mathematics and Computation 509 (2026). doi:10.1016/j.amc.2025.129638\n\nBibTeX entries are in CITATION.bib.","title":"Citing"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#Troubleshooting","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#The-first-call-is-slow","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"That is Julia compiling code for your model. It happens once for each model in a session, and the second call to estimate shows the real speed.","title":"The first call is slow"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#It-is-still-running","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Pass progress = true to see which stage it is in. If it is working through the interpolators one after another, a shorter interpolators list or fewer shooting_points will cut the time. See Going faster. Large models can take a long time in any case. See Size.","title":"It is still running"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#The-estimates-are-off","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Simulate the model with the estimates and plot it against the data, as in Seeing the fit. Then:\n\nThe curve misses the data. With noisy data, more points help. See Noisy data. If the data are clean and the curve still misses, the model probably does not describe them.\nThe curve fits, but the values are not what you expected. The data may allow more than one answer. Look at the other results, and at whether any values are marked not identifiable. See More than one answer.","title":"The estimates are off"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#There-are-several-results,-or-values-marked-\"not-identifiable\"","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Both are the package telling you something about the model, and More than one answer explains what.","title":"There are several results, or values marked \"not identifiable\""},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#There-are-no-results","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"estimate returned an empty vector: none of the solutions of the equations held up when checked against the data. Check that the time points and values are what you meant to pass, and that the model can produce curves like them. Running with nooutput = false prints what each stage found.","title":"There are no results"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#\"This-model-is-not-rational\"","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"The model applies a function such as sin or exp to a state or an unknown parameter. Which models work says what is allowed and how such a model can sometimes be rewritten.","title":"\"This model is not rational\""},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#\"This-model-needs-derivative-…-of-the-measured-data\"","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Too few quantities are measured for the number of unknowns. Measuring one more state usually brings the required derivatives down.","title":"\"This model needs derivative … of the measured data\""},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#The-results-change-from-run-to-run","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Several steps use random numbers. Pass a seed. See Repeatable results.","title":"The results change from run to run"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/troubleshooting/#Something-else","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Please open an issue with the model and, if you can share it, the data. A simulated data set that shows the same behavior is just as useful.","title":"Something else"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/uncertainty/#Uncertainty","page":"Uncertainty (experimental)","text":"warning: Experimental\nUncertainty estimates are experimental. They have been tested on only a few models, and it is not yet known how well they hold up in general. If you try them, please open an issue and say how they did. Good or bad, that is useful to hear.\n\nWith noisy data an estimate is only good to within some margin. The package can estimate that margin, as a standard error for each parameter and initial condition.\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named oscillator = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x2, D(x2) ~ b * x1], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(oscillator, [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x2];\n    true_values = [a => 0.4, b => 0.8, x1 => 1.0, x2 => 0.5])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem;\n    datasize = 101, time_interval = [0.0, 5.0], noise_level = 0.01, seed = 1)\n\nTwo options turn it on. compute_uncertainty = true asks for it. InterpolatorAGPUQ is the curve fit that makes it possible: a Gaussian process that estimates the noise in the data along with the curve. The uncertainty comes back as the third value of analyze_parameter_estimation_problem:\n\noptions = EstimationOptions(interpolators = [InterpolatorAGPUQ], compute_uncertainty = true, seed = 1)\nraw, analysis, uncertainty = analyze_parameter_estimation_problem(problem, options)\n\nfor (name, value, standard_error) in zip(uncertainty.param_labels, uncertainty.estimate_values, uncertainty.param_std)\n    println(rpad(name, 4), round(value; sigdigits = 4), \" ± \", round(standard_error; sigdigits = 2))\nend\na   0.4007 ± 0.00049\nb   0.7976 ± 0.0019\nx1  1.001 ± 0.00083\nx2  0.5022 ± 0.0021\n\nThe data were simulated with a = 0.4, b = 0.8, x1 = 1 and x2 = 0.5, and each estimate is within a standard error or two of its true value.\n\nuncertainty.param_covariance is the full covariance matrix, and print_uncertainty_results(uncertainty) prints a longer report.","title":"Uncertainty"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/uncertainty/#What-the-numbers-mean","page":"Uncertainty (experimental)","text":"The Gaussian process learns how noisy the data are. That noise is then carried through the least-squares fit to the parameters, using a linear approximation around the estimate. The standard errors say how far the estimate would move if the same experiment were measured again with fresh noise.\n\nKeep three things in mind:\n\nThey belong to the first result. If the model has several answers, they say nothing about the others.\nThey rest on a linear approximation. With little noise it is good. With a lot of noise, or a model that reacts sharply to its parameters, treat them as a guide to the size of the error, not as exact confidence intervals.\nThey assume the model is right. If it does not describe the system, small standard errors do not make the estimates meaningful.","title":"What the numbers mean"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/uncertainty/#When-it-cannot-be-computed","page":"Uncertainty (experimental)","text":"If the uncertainty cannot be worked out, the third value is a UQUnavailable in place of a report, and its message says why:\n\nif uncertainty isa UncertaintyReport\n    uncertainty.param_std\nelse\n    println(uncertainty.message)\nend\n\nA report also carries its own checks. uncertainty.status is :ok when they passed, and uncertainty.warnings lists the assumptions behind the numbers.","title":"When it cannot be computed"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Which-models-work","page":"Which models work","text":"","title":"Which models work"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#The-rule","page":"Which models work","text":"The right-hand sides of the model and the measured quantities have to be rational: built from the states, the parameters and numbers using +, -, *, / and whole-number powers.\n\nThat covers a lot of ground:\n\nmass-action chemistry, and anything else made of products of concentrations\npopulation and epidemic models such as Lotka–Volterra, SIR and SEIR\ncompartment models from pharmacokinetics\nsaturating terms such as V * x / (K + x), and Hill terms with whole-number exponents\nlinear systems\n\nParameters can go wherever states can: multiplied together, in denominators, and in the measured quantities.","title":"The rule"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Inputs-that-depend-on-time","page":"Which models work","text":"sin(c * t), cos(c * t) and exp(c * t) are accepted when c is a number. They depend on time alone, so they are known in advance:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x(t) y(t)\n@named forced = System([D(x) ~ -a * x + b * sin(0.5 * t)], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(forced, [y ~ x]; true_values = [a => 0.5, b => 2.0, x => 1.0])\nproblem = sample_problem_data(problem; datasize = 51, time_interval = [0.0, 10.0])\nestimate(problem)[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.5\n    b = 2\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x(t) = 1\n  Fit error: 8.85e-26","title":"Inputs that depend on time"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#What-is-not-accepted","page":"Which models work","text":"Any other function of a state or of an unknown parameter: sin(θ), exp(-E / T), sqrt(h), log(x). The package refuses such a model before it does any work:\n\n@parameters g\n@variables θ(t) ω(t)\n@named pendulum = System([D(θ) ~ ω, D(ω) ~ -g * sin(θ)], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(pendulum, [y ~ θ]; data = (t = [0.0, 0.1, 0.2], y = [0.5, 0.49, 0.46]))\ntry\n    estimate(problem)\ncatch err\n    showerror(stdout, err)\nend\nThis model is not rational: it contains sin(θ(t)). A model must be built from polynomials and ratios of polynomials in its states and parameters. sin(c*t), cos(c*t) and exp(c*t) with a number c are accepted.\n\nThree more things are outside the rule: a frequency that is itself unknown, as in sin(w * t), a power with an unknown exponent, and time on its own, as in b * t.","title":"What is not accepted"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Rewriting-a-model-so-that-it-is-accepted","page":"Which models work","text":"Many models that break the rule can be rewritten to follow it by adding states. The idea is always the same: give the offending term a name, write down its derivative, and tell the package its values.\n\nA function of a measured state. In the pendulum, call sin(θ) and cos(θ) by the names s and c. Their derivatives are c * ω and -s * ω, which are polynomial. Since θ is measured, s and c are as good as measured too: their data are the sine and cosine of the angle data.\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\n\n# Angles of a pendulum with g = 9.8, released from rest at 0.5 radians.\ntimes = collect(range(0, 2; length = 81))\nswing = solve(ODEProblem((u, p, t) -> [u[2], -9.8 * sin(u[1])], [0.5, 0.0], (0.0, 2.0)), Vern9(); saveat = times, abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12)\nangles = swing[1, :]\n\n@variables s(t) c(t) ys(t) yc(t)\n@named pendulum = System([D(θ) ~ ω, D(ω) ~ -g * s, D(s) ~ c * ω, D(c) ~ -s * ω], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(pendulum, [y ~ θ, ys ~ s, yc ~ c];\n    data = (t = times, y = angles, ys = sin.(angles), yc = cos.(angles)))\nestimate(problem)[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    g = 9.8\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    θ(t) = 0.5\n    ω(t) = 1.22689e-14\n    s(t) = 0.479426\n    c(t) = 0.877583\n  Fit error: 1.91e-25\n\nTime itself. Add a state τ with D(τ) ~ 1 and use it wherever the model has a bare t. Its data are the time points:\n\n@variables τ(t) yτ(t)\n@named ramp = System([D(x) ~ -a * x + b * τ, D(τ) ~ 1], t)\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(ramp, [y ~ x, yτ ~ τ];\n    data = (t = times, y = measurements, yτ = times))","title":"Rewriting a model so that it is accepted"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#What-has-to-be-measured","page":"Which models work","text":"Not every state needs to be measured. The states you do not measure are estimated along with the parameters, as initial conditions. What matters is that every unknown leaves a trace in what you measure. One that leaves none is reported as not identifiable. See More than one answer.\n\nThe fewer quantities you measure, the higher the derivatives of the data the method needs, and high derivatives are hard to get from noisy data. A model that needs more than the twentieth derivative is refused.","title":"What has to be measured"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Size","page":"Which models work","text":"The cost grows quickly with the number of unknowns, and it depends on the shape of the equations more than on the amount of data. Models with up to about a dozen parameters are the usual range. Larger ones can work, and take much longer. Example models lists some of each kind.","title":"Size"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/types/#Other-types","page":"Other types","text":"The package exports many more names than everyday use needs: the individual interpolators and solvers, the stages of the estimation, and the tools used to study how it behaves. This page and the next list them as they are documented in the source. The names meant for everyday use are on the previous page.\n\nThe types to do with uncertainty, UncertaintyReport and those around it, belong to an experimental part of the package. See Uncertainty.","title":"Other types"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.AAADapprox","page":"Other types","text":"AAADapprox{T} <: AbstractInterpolator\n\nInterpolator using AAA algorithm from BaryRational package.\n\nFields\n\ninternalAAA::T: Internal AAA representation from BaryRational\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.AAADapprox"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.AGPInterpolator","page":"Other types","text":"AGPInterpolator <: AbstractInterpolator\n\nGP interpolator using AbstractGPs.jl with uncertainty quantification support.\n\nFields\n\nmean_function::Function: Returns mean prediction (denormalized)\nstd_function::Function: Returns standard deviation (denormalized)\nposterior::Any: AbstractGPs posterior GP object\nx_min::Float64: Minimum x value (for normalization)\nx_range::Float64: Range of x values (for normalization)\ny_mean::Float64: Mean of y values (for denormalization)\ny_std::Float64: Std of y values (for denormalization)\n\nUsage\n\nWhen called as interp(x), returns mean prediction only (drop-in compatible). For uncertainty, use mean_and_var(interp, x) to get (mean, variance) tuple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.AGPInterpolator"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.AGPInterpolatorUQ","page":"Other types","text":"AGPInterpolatorUQ\n\nExtended GP interpolator with full uncertainty quantification support. Stores pre-computed values needed for efficient derivative covariance computation.\n\nFields\n\nmean_function::Function: Returns mean prediction (denormalized)\nstd_function::Function: Returns standard deviation (denormalized)\nxs_train::Vector{Float64}: Training x values (raw, not normalized)\nys_train::Vector{Float64}: Training y values (normalized)\nalpha::Vector{Float64}: Pre-computed weights (K⁻¹ y)\nchol::Cholesky: Cholesky factorization of K + σₙ²I\nlengthscale::Float64: Optimized lengthscale\nfitted_lengthscale::Float64: Function-value marginal-likelihood lengthscale\nlengthscale_factor::Float64: Explicit derivative-oriented multiplier\nsignal_var::Float64: Optimized signal variance (σ²)\nnoise_var::Float64: Optimized noise variance (σₙ²)\ny_mean::Float64: Mean of original y values\ny_std::Float64: Std of original y values\ncholesky_jitter::Float64: Absolute diagonal regularizer used by final factorization\ncholesky_jitter_ratio::Float64: Jitter divided by learned observation-noise variance\nfactorization_residual::Float64: Relative residual of the retained Cholesky factor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.AGPInterpolatorUQ"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.AbstractInterpolator","page":"Other types","text":"AbstractInterpolator\n\nAbstract type for interpolation function objects. All interpolators should be callable with a single argument and return the interpolated value.\n\nDefined here (not in core/derivatives.jl) so the type exists before any file that annotates with it — parameter_estimation.jl (included earlier than derivatives.jl) references Dict{Num, AbstractInterpolator}, which previously survived only by lazy in-function evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.AbstractInterpolator"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.AbstractUQOutcome","page":"Other types","text":"Common supertype for requested uncertainty-quantification outcomes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.AbstractUQOutcome"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.ChebyshevApprox","page":"Other types","text":"ChebyshevApprox <: AbstractInterpolator\n\nChebyshev polynomial least-squares fit with automatic degree selection. Derivatives are exact via Chebyshev coefficient recurrence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ChebyshevApprox"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.DerivativeData","page":"Other types","text":"DerivativeData\n\nStruct to store derivative data of state variable equations and measured quantity equations. No substitutions are made. The \"cleared\" versions are produced from versions of the state equations and measured quantity equations which have had their denominators cleared, i.e. they should be polynomial and never rational. Their tables may be empty or shorter than the rational tables when construction is deferred. Call ensure_cleared_derivatives! before reading a required cleared order.\n\nFields\n\nstates_lhs_cleared::Vector{Vector{Num}}: Left-hand side of cleared state equations (indexed by [derivative_order+1])\nstates_rhs_cleared::Vector{Vector{Num}}: Right-hand side of cleared state equations\nobs_lhs_cleared::Vector{Vector{Num}}: Left-hand side of cleared observation equations\nobs_rhs_cleared::Vector{Vector{Num}}: Right-hand side of cleared observation equations\nstates_lhs::Vector{Vector{Num}}: Left-hand side of state equations\nstates_rhs::Vector{Vector{Num}}: Right-hand side of state equations\nobs_lhs::Vector{Vector{Num}}: Left-hand side of observation equations\nobs_rhs::Vector{Vector{Num}}: Right-hand side of observation equations\nall_unidentifiable::Set{Num}: Set of all unidentifiable parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.DerivativeData"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.DerivativeUncertaintyEstimate","page":"Other types","text":"DerivativeUncertaintyEstimate\n\nPer-(observable index, derivative order) uncertainty σd at a single evaluation time `teval. Σ_d for downstream covariance propagation isdiag(σd²)flattened in the same order as the data labels returned bycomputedatasensitivity`.\n\nFields\n\nt_eval::Float64 — the time at which σ_d is evaluated.\nn_observables::Int, max_order::Int — shape of the σ_d matrix.\nsigma_d::Matrix{Float64} — sigma_d[obs_idx, order+1] is the uncertainty estimate for the order-th derivative of observable obs_idx at t_eval.\nsource_values::Matrix{Vector{Tuple{Symbol, Float64}}} — for each (obs_idx, order+1), the list of (interpolator source, derivative value) pairs that contributed. Useful for diagnostic inspection.\nsources_used::Vector{Symbol} — interpolator sources actually consulted.\nfloor_active::Matrix{Bool} — true where the order-decay floor exceeded the cross-interpolator spread; signals \"we believe derivatives are at least this uncertain regardless of interpolator agreement.\"\nnoise_level::Float64, order_decay_kappa::Float64 — provenance for the floor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.DerivativeUncertaintyEstimate"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.EstimationFlow","page":"Other types","text":"EstimationFlow\n\nEnum selecting the high-level workflow used during estimation.\n\nFlowStandard: New optimized multishot workflow (default)\nFlowDirectOpt: Direct local optimization workflow (LBFGS from random start)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.EstimationFlow"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.EstimatorIdentity","page":"Other types","text":"EstimatorIdentity\n\nCanonical, run-scoped identity of the estimator that produced one candidate. Unlike the legacy flat provenance fields, this record describes the estimator that produced the CURRENT values and preserves parent IDs whenever a later stage transforms a candidate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.EstimatorIdentity"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.FHDapprox","page":"Other types","text":"FHDapprox{T} <: AbstractInterpolator\n\nInterpolator using Floater-Hormann algorithm from BaryRational package.\n\nFields\n\ninternalFHD::T: Internal Floater-Hormann representation from BaryRational\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.FHDapprox"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.GPRapprox","page":"Other types","text":"GPRapprox <: AbstractInterpolator\n\nInterpolator using Gaussian Process Regression.\n\nFields\n\ngp_function::Function: Callable function for evaluating the GPR prediction\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.GPRapprox"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.InterpolatorMethod","page":"Other types","text":"InterpolatorMethod\n\nEnum for selecting the data interpolation method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.InterpolatorMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.JetInfluenceEstimate","page":"Other types","text":"JetInfluenceEstimate\n\nLinear influence representation for observable jets estimated from data.\n\nFor fixed GP hyperparameters, the derivative estimator is linear in the raw observations: jet_mean = W * y.  The estimator sampling covariance is then jet_covariance = W * observation_covariance * W'.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.JetInfluenceEstimate"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.LocalUQSnapshot","page":"Other types","text":"LocalUQSnapshot\n\nAudit copy of the direct IFT uncertainty calculation in the local algebraic coordinates at t_eval.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.LocalUQSnapshot"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.MultiPointEvaluation","page":"Other types","text":"MultiPointEvaluation\n\nResult of evaluating a MultiPointTemplate at specific time point indices. Contains the data variable values needed for HC.jl parameter substitution or homotopy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.MultiPointEvaluation"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.MultiPointTemplate","page":"Other types","text":"MultiPointTemplate\n\nPre-computed multi-point polynomial template. Built once per model from the single-point SI template. Stores the structural recipe (which equations to keep) so that evaluation at specific time points is cheap.\n\nThe template stores equations in symbolic form (data variables not substituted), enabling HC.jl parameter homotopy across different time point pairs.\n\nInvariant: length(stripped_equations) == length(solve_vars) (square system).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.MultiPointTemplate"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.NumericalIdentifiabilityAdvisory","page":"Other types","text":"NumericalIdentifiabilityAdvisory\n\nStructured best-effort numerical identifiability diagnostics.\n\nFields\n\nstatus::Symbol: :available, :failed, or :unavailable\nrecommended_num_points::Union{Nothing, Int}: Recommended point count heuristic\nrecommended_deriv_level::Dict{Int, Int}: Recommended derivative depth by observable index\nflagged_variables::Set{Num}: Variables flagged as numerically fragile at the probe point\nnotes::Vector{Symbol}: Advisory notes such as :heuristic_fallback\nfailure_reason::Union{Nothing, String}: Best-effort failure summary when advisory analysis failed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.NumericalIdentifiabilityAdvisory"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.OrderedODESystem","page":"Other types","text":"OrderedODESystem\n\nA ModelingToolkit system together with its parameters and states in a fixed order. Results list their values in this order.\n\nThe keyword constructor of ParameterEstimationProblem builds one from a System, so you rarely need to.\n\nFields\n\nsystem: the ModelingToolkit system.\noriginal_parameters: the parameters, in order.\noriginal_states: the states, in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.OrderedODESystem"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.PerfectInterpolant","page":"Other types","text":"PerfectInterpolant\n\nStores Taylor coefficients c[k+1] = f^(k)(t0) / k! and evaluates as a polynomial via Horner's method.  TaylorDiff on this interpolant yields machine-precision derivatives up to the stored order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.PerfectInterpolant"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.PolishMethod","page":"Other types","text":"PolishMethod\n\nEnum for selecting the optimization method for solution polishing. Note: The scalar methods are Optim.jl optimizers run through Optimization.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.PolishMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.PracticalIdentifiabilityIndex","page":"Other types","text":"PracticalIdentifiabilityIndex\n\nScale-normalized covariance summary for the selected physical unknowns.\n\ni_a = sqrt(lambda_max(normalized_covariance)), where normalized_covariance = D^{-1} P Σ_x P' D^{-1}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.PracticalIdentifiabilityIndex"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.ResultProvenance","page":"Other types","text":"ResultProvenance\n\nStructured lineage metadata for a parameter-estimation result.\n\nFields\n\nprimary_method::Symbol: :algebraic or :direct_opt\ninterpolator_source::Union{Nothing, Symbol}: Which interpolator produced the candidate\nrescue_path::Symbol: :none, :algebraic_resolve_t0, :algebraic_resolve_seeded, :direct_opt_fallback\nsource_shooting_index::Union{Nothing, Int}: Shooting-point index that produced the candidate\nsource_candidate_index::Union{Nothing, Int}: Stable candidate index within the producing phase\npre_polish_error::Union{Nothing, Float64}: Error before polishing, when applicable\npost_polish_error::Union{Nothing, Float64}: Error after polishing, when applicable\npolish_applied::Bool: Whether a polishing stage was applied\nrepresentative_assignments::OrderedDict{Num, Float64}: Values assigned only because variables were already known structurally unidentifiable\nstructural_fix_set::OrderedDict{Num, Float64}: Representative structural fix set derived from SI structural outputs\ntemplate_status::Union{Nothing, Symbol}: Template dimension status after structural fixing (2026-08: replaced the vestigial residualfixset + before/after status pair — no residual-repair mechanism ever existed, so the pair was identical by construction)\nequations_dropped_by_rank_trimming::Vector{Int}: Equation indices removed by rank-based template trimming\npractical_identifiability_status::Symbol: Practical/numerical identifiability assessment status for this flow\nnumerical_advisory::Union{Nothing, NumericalIdentifiabilityAdvisory}: Best-effort advisory numerical diagnostics and heuristic recommendations\nnotes::Vector{Symbol}: Additional lineage/debug notes\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ResultProvenance"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.ScaleInfo","page":"Other types","text":"ScaleInfo\n\nPower-of-2 scale factors chosen for a rescaled ParameterEstimationProblem.\n\nFields\n\nstate_scales::OrderedDict{Num, Float64}: state x_i => 2^{a_i} (xioriginal = scale * xiscaled)\nparam_scales::OrderedDict{Num, Float64}: parameter p_j => 2^{b_j}\nobservable_scales::OrderedDict{Any, Float64}: datasample key (Num(mq.rhs)) => `2^{ok}`\nmetadata::NamedTuple: diagnostics (max_const_before, max_const_after, n_nontrivial, ...)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.ScaleInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.SensitivitySeedReport","page":"Other types","text":"SensitivitySeedReport\n\nProvenance + diagnostics for a sensitivity-seed generation run. Returned by generate_sensitivity_seeds so the caller can log what was emitted and why.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.SensitivitySeedReport"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.StackedJetInfluenceEstimate","page":"Other types","text":"StackedJetInfluenceEstimate\n\nMulti-time sibling of JetInfluenceEstimate: the estimator-sampling covariance of one observable's posterior-mean jets at SEVERAL evaluation times, factorized as W_stack · Σ_y · W_stackᵀ. Cross-time blocks are nonzero because every jet is a linear functional of the SAME training observations. PSD by construction; genuine singularity (e.g. repeated times) is preserved rather than repaired.\n\nRow order is time-major: for each time in t_evals, orders 0:max_deriv. Use stacked_jet_index to map (time_idx, order) → row/column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.StackedJetInfluenceEstimate"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.SystemSolverMethod","page":"Other types","text":"SystemSolverMethod\n\nEnum for selecting the polynomial system solver method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.SystemSolverMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.TranscendentalInfo","page":"Other types","text":"TranscendentalInfo\n\nStores all information about transcendental functions detected in an ODE system.\n\nFields\n\nentries::Vector{TranscendentalEntry}: All detected transcendental subexpressions\nsubstitution_dict::Dict{Num, Num}: Maps original expr → input variable\nreverse_dict::Dict{Num, Num}: Maps input variable → original expr\ninput_variables::Vector{Num}: All input variables created\nmax_derivative_order::Int: Maximum derivative order pre-computed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.TranscendentalInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.UQBacksolveTransform","page":"Other types","text":"UQBacksolveTransform\n\nLinear delta-method map from local coordinates [p, x(t_eval)] to physical coordinates [p, x(t0)].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UQBacksolveTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.UQLinearizationDiagnostics","page":"Other types","text":"Numerical audit values for the local estimator linearization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UQLinearizationDiagnostics"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.UQReliabilityAssessment","page":"Other types","text":"UQReliabilityAssessment\n\nOrthogonal reliability axes for a UQ outcome.  These deliberately do not collapse software availability, local numerical validity, interval width, selection conditioning, and empirical calibration into a single status flag. UncertaintyReport.status remains as a backward-compatible summary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UQReliabilityAssessment"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.UQTargetSnapshot","page":"Other types","text":"UQTargetSnapshot\n\nSerializable audit record for the exact rank-one estimator targeted by UQ. lineage contains the selected identity followed by any retained ancestors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UQTargetSnapshot"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.UQUnavailable","page":"Other types","text":"Typed outcome returned when UQ was requested but cannot be computed honestly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UQUnavailable"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/types/#ODEParameterEstimation.UncertaintyReport","page":"Other types","text":"UncertaintyReport\n\nPropagated parameter uncertainty through the implicit function theorem sensitivity matrix.\n\nΣx = S · Σd · S' where S = -(∂F/∂x)⁻¹·(∂F/∂d).  For the default package UQ, Σ_d is the estimator sampling covariance of the observable jet, conditional on learned GP hyperparameters.  Legacy latent GP posterior covariance is kept as a separate diagnostic concept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODEParameterEstimation.UncertaintyReport"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#Your-own-data","page":"Your own data","text":"To estimate from measurements you already have, hand them to the problem as data. Nothing else changes.","title":"Your own data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#Measurements-in-vectors","page":"Your own data","text":"Take a model with two states, both measured, and two unknown rates:\n\nusing ODEParameterEstimation, ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters a b\n@variables x1(t) x2(t) y1(t) y2(t)\n@named oscillator = System([D(x1) ~ -a * x2, D(x2) ~ b * x1], t)\nmeasured = [y1 ~ x1, y2 ~ x2]\n\nMeasurements are a vector of times and one vector of values for each measured quantity. These three stand in for yours:\n\ntimes = collect(range(0, 5; length = 41))\nseries1 = cos.(0.6 .* times .+ 0.3)\nseries2 = 1.5 .* sin.(0.6 .* times .+ 0.3)\n\nGive them under the names on the left-hand sides of the measured quantities, with the times under t:\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(oscillator, measured;\n    data = (t = times, y1 = series1, y2 = series2))\n\nresults = estimate(problem)\nresults[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.4\n    b = 0.9\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 0.955336\n    x2(t) = 0.44328\n  Fit error: 3.16e-26\n\nThose two curves solve the model for a = 0.4 and b = 0.9, which is what came back, along with their values at the first time point.","title":"Measurements in vectors"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#Measurements-in-a-table","page":"Your own data","text":"data can be anything with named columns, such as a DataFrame read from a file:\n\nusing CSV, DataFrames\n\ntable = CSV.read(\"measurements.csv\", DataFrame)   # columns t, y1 and y2\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(oscillator, measured; data = table)\n\nOther columns are ignored, and the rows do not have to be in time order. A dictionary works too: Dict(\"t\" => times, \"y1\" => series1, \"y2\" => series2).\n\nEvery series needs a value at every time. If the table has gaps, either drop those rows (dropmissing(table)) or describe each series separately, as below.","title":"Measurements in a table"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#Series-measured-at-different-times","page":"Your own data","text":"When the quantities were not measured at the same times, describe each series on its own with an ObservationSeries and collect them in an ObservationData:\n\ntimes1 = collect(range(0, 5; length = 41))\ntimes2 = collect(range(0.1, 4.9; length = 33))\n\ndata = ObservationData([\n    ObservationSeries(\"y1\", \"run 1\", x1, times1, cos.(0.6 .* times1 .+ 0.3)),\n    ObservationSeries(\"y2\", \"run 1\", x2, times2, 1.5 .* sin.(0.6 .* times2 .+ 0.3)),\n])\n\nproblem = ParameterEstimationProblem(oscillator, measured; data)\nestimate(problem)[1]\nParameterEstimationResult\n  Parameters\n    a = 0.4\n    b = 0.9\n  Initial conditions (t = 0)\n    x1(t) = 0.955336\n    x2(t) = 0.44328\n  Fit error: 3e-26\n\nEach series takes a name, a name for the experiment it belongs to, the quantity that was measured, its times and its values. The quantity is the right-hand side of the measured quantity, x1 and not y1. Initial conditions are reported at time zero unless you pass initial_time to ObservationData.","title":"Series measured at different times"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#How-much-data-is-enough","page":"Your own data","text":"The method works from the shape of each curve, so it needs enough points to see that shape: a few dozen per series, spread over a stretch of time in which the system is doing something. A long tail where everything has settled adds little.","title":"How much data is enough"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#If-the-measurements-are-noisy","page":"Your own data","text":"The example above used exact numbers. Real measurements are not exact, and estimate allows for that: it fits its answers to the data before returning them. Noisy data shows how much that matters, and what else helps.","title":"If the measurements are noisy"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/own_data/#Checking-the-result","page":"Your own data","text":"A result holds numbers, not a judgement of whether the model is right. Simulate the model with the estimates and look at it against the measurements, as in Seeing the fit. If the curve misses the data, the model does not describe them, whatever the parameters.","title":"Checking the result"}]
}
